WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.964
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.930
LORENZ: Indeed, a pleasure to
introduce to you this afternoon

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.770
Dr. Benoit Mandelbrot,
who presently

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:23.160
is Sterling professor of
mathematical sciences at Yale

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:28.000
University and research fellow
emeritus at the IBM Thomas J.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.450
Watson Research Center.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:34.470
Professor Mandelbrot is truly
a man from many nations.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:38.700
He was born in Warsaw, Poland
and in the second decade moved

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.100
to France, where he
subsequently received

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.440
his doctorate from the
Faculty of Sciences

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.160
at the University of Paris.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.790
And he has spent most of
the latter half of his life

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.920
in this country.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.480
Many people, however,
associate him

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.600
more closely with another
country, Great Britain,

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.400
because he asked
in the title of one

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.700
of his widely-known papers, "How
Long is the Coast of Britain?"

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.650
Here, he points out
that the coastline

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:05.780
is self-similar
under a magnification

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.690
so that its measured
length will depend

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.870
upon the length of
the measuring stick.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:13.030
The shorter the stick,
the longer the coast.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:17.250
And, indeed, if it were not for
the incessant waves and tide

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:20.080
so an arbitrarily short
stick could be used,

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.030
the length could go to infinity.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:27.060
His continuing pursuit of the
phenomenon of self-similarity,

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.690
which he has recognized
not just in coastlines,

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:32.840
but in a wide variety
of natural systems,

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.930
ultimately led him
to coin a word that

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:38.960
is now familiar to nearly
everyone, fractals.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.340
And today, his
lecture title will

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.580
be Fractals in Science,
Engineering, and Finance,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.010
Roughness and Beauty.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:50.250
His list of honorary degrees
and medals and other honors

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.250
is far too long for
me to have memorized.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.590
I could read it to you,
but this would cut deeply

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.620
into the speaking time
that is rightfully his.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.140
So if you need further
evidence of the stature

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.660
that he has attained,
simply look around

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.740
at the crowd that had filled
every seat 10 minutes ago.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.586
Without further ado then, let me
introduce Professor Mandelbrot.

00:02:11.586 --> 00:02:13.570
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:25.130
MANDELBROT: It is, indeed, a
delight to be here among you

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:29.360
and, in particular, a delight
to be introduced by Ed Lorenz.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.920
We met actually around the
'64 when a mutual friend Erik

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.310
Mollo-Christensen
told me, well, there

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:42.960
is another man in dog house not
quite yours, but a bit further.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:48.180
And so you two lost souls may
find some interests in common.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.010
Well, Erik was very,
very, very clear-sighted.

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:57.200
Because, indeed, the work
of Professor Lorenz and mine

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:02.330
have very many points in common,
even though they never quite

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:04.960
[INAUDIBLE] the same field.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:06.800
Now, the title you
see on the screen

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.900
here is one which,
in a certain sense,

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.970
summarizes my whole life.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:14.180
I've been writing papers
for about 50 years, which is

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:16.200
a fairly long period of time.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:20.420
And as time went on, many
people asked themselves

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:24.230
what was the leading
thought which

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:28.130
permeated all my
changes of interest

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:29.810
from one field to another.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:33.680
And frankly, my answers
were rather cumbersome.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.840
It's only lately that I
realized that everything

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.040
was very simple.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.930
All my life I've been working
towards what may perhaps

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:44.930
become a sphere of roughness.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:48.740
Now, let me describe this
phenomenon of roughness.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:53.900
If we think of which shapes
in our ordinary evidence

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:59.170
are smooth, well, there are
very few-- a plane, perhaps,

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:03.890
for a child, a primitive man,
a child once upon a time.

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:08.120
A quiet piece of
water without wind.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:15.200
A circle-- well, full moon, the
pupil in the iris of the eye,

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.160
a few more shapes like that.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:18.910
Straight lines-- very few.

00:04:21.899 --> 00:04:24.090
On the other side,
the rough shapes

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.040
are absolutely without number.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.690
Wherever we look,
we see shapes which

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.830
are very, very complicated.

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:33.630
Now, the history of
science, as I see today

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.910
after experience
in many sciences,

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:39.150
very definitely continues to
be dominated by its origin.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:42.030
And science began with
our sensations, senses--

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:47.190
the eye, the ear, the feeling
of hot, the feeling of heavy,

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:48.570
the feeling of
rough, the feeling

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:51.420
of sweet or sour or acid.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:55.920
And each of these senses except
one gave rise in due time,

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.410
early on actually,
into a science.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:01.420
More precisely, each went
through three stages.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:04.080
For example, in acoustics,
the idea of sound

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:09.000
was known from time immemorial
since music always existed.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.890
And scales were known, also.

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:14.340
But the association of
sound with frequency

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.010
is the beginning of
acoustics and developed

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:18.580
in a very strong fashion.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.670
It developed mostly
by a procedure

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:22.320
which I'm trying
to describe, which

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.030
is very important I think.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.550
Acoustics did not try
to represent all sounds.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:27.720
That's impossible.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:32.880
It focused on the sound of
idealized strings or pipes

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.370
and did a marvelous
job with them.

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:38.070
For drums, for concert
halls, acoustics

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.800
is not so terribly perfect.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.930
But that is not a criticism.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:44.760
A science does not
have to be perfect.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.640
A science must
find in one aspect

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:52.080
of the mess of our senses
some substantial part

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.660
for which a theory can be done.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.180
And this theory must
also give insights

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:01.050
about the cases to which
it does not strictly apply.

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:04.320
And at this point in
time, much of the effort

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:08.160
of many scientists is
still, naturally enough,

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.820
devoted to developing those
senses which I described

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:13.770
and also, of course,
to link them.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:16.890
For example--
unified field theory

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.140
proposed to link the
feelings of heavy

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.870
and the feeling of
light, light and weight.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:26.160
But in all this
extraordinary effort,

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:28.800
one sense has become
totally deprived

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.630
of implementation in science.

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:33.960
And that's the
sense of roughness.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.270
The problem goes
very, very far back.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:41.040
Being very much one of those
nuts who read the old books,

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:45.490
I was very impressed to find
in Plato, one of his dialogues,

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:47.590
a list of the senses
and what I just said,

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:49.450
the sciences
corresponding to it.

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:51.120
And then he says, roughness is--

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.880
well, he didn't
say much about it.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.880
And roughness, indeed, turns
out to be far more complicated

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:01.270
than the other sciences
to require mathematics

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:06.030
which is a markedly higher
level of complication

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:08.110
end of difficulty.

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:14.130
So what my whole life has aimed
at was to find irregularities,

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:16.980
invariances-- since
science is only study

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:20.370
of invariances-- invariances
in our experience of roughness,

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:24.120
which might apply throughout
and might provide tools

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:27.630
for attacking roughness
in all its aspects.

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:32.340
So when I say that fractals--
which is the word I coined

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:35.380
for a certain kind of
orderly roughness--

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:37.130
they occur in mathematics,
I will give you

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:38.510
at least one example.

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:41.100
The sciences I will give you
a few examples in passing--

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:42.380
engineering, finance.

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:44.390
I'll spend some time in finance.

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:49.260
Now, sometimes one wonders
why all these various topics

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:50.839
are brought together.

00:07:50.839 --> 00:07:53.130
They're not brought together
for any fundamental reason

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:56.100
except that they all
exemplify, once again,

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.350
these very simple and
fundamental issue,

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:00.420
the question of roughness.

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:04.130
So as I see on the
first line, fractals

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:06.150
are simple, complex,
and open-ended.

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:08.050
What do I mean by that?

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:12.960
If one begins to define fractals
without the kind of cloud

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:18.990
of sophistication, rigor, and
mathematical elusiveness which

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:23.010
was given to them for quite
a while when they are purely

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:25.590
part of mathematics,
if one forgets

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:27.580
about these complications,
axiom is simple.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:32.700
In fact, children are taught
the ideas very, very early on.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:37.200
But these very simple rules
create extraordinarily complex

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:43.020
reality and very rapidly,
as I will show you shortly.

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:48.250
And it's open-ended in a sense
that one very rapidly goes

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:51.690
to problems of such
extraordinary difficulty

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:56.920
that the best minds are
struggling to cover them.

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:00.570
Now, everybody knows
me for pictures.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:03.340
And for some, pictures
were quite glamorous.

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:06.100
I'm very, very happy about them.

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:10.800
I'm delighted that an
instrument like the computer,

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:12.960
which at that time
was devoted entirely

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:16.900
to arithmetic
operations and the like,

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:19.050
had been tamed for the
purpose so different

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:21.150
from its original purpose.

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:24.720
But my experience is that if I
started showing you pictures,

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:25.980
I will never end.

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:30.300
Great fun for you for me,
but it's out of place.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:33.300
So I will simply
not dwell on them.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:34.893
I will just show you this one.

00:09:34.893 --> 00:09:37.158
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:42.530
Well, let's now get to my point.

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:45.200
What is roughness
and how to handle it?

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.780
Well, it's very appropriate
that this auditorium

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:49.460
is in a building
of Earth Sciences,

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:52.220
because an Earth
scientist, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:53.540
gave me this picture.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:56.840
He told me that when he teaches
geology to his students,

00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:00.390
he's very careful to tell them
at every given time always put

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:01.610
them into scale.

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:03.950
Because if you don't
put them into scale,

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:05.990
you'll forget how
big the thing was

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:07.880
after [INAUDIBLE] photographed.

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:10.700
So you put yourself, the
camera cap, or something in it.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:13.010
And then you remember
how big it is.

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:14.000
Otherwise, you forget.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.140
You confuse a pile of
rocks and a mountain.

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:20.240
Well, here is an
example, indeed--

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:21.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:22.787
--of this confusion.

00:10:22.787 --> 00:10:23.620
This one's cheating.

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:25.310
It's very easy to cheat.

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:28.580
Because, indeed,
this type of terrain

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:30.330
is the same at all scales.

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:33.650
Now, to be the same at all
scales is, at this level,

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:35.313
an element of purely folklore.

00:10:35.313 --> 00:10:39.870
And I'll hasten to go ahead and
speak of finance, since I spent

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:42.800
many, many years of my
life both in the '60s

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:47.840
and again recently in
studying financial crisis.

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:49.890
In finance, there is
also a piece of folklore

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:53.600
which says that, if you put
a price graph somewhere,

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:55.460
be careful to put
a scale of time.

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:57.140
Because, otherwise,
one would not

00:10:57.140 --> 00:10:59.690
know whether the price
changes or price is

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:01.440
every minute, every second--

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:04.430
well, that's recent-- or
every day, or every year.

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:07.640
It's all the same, goes up
and down, zigzags and so on.

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:09.230
This is folklore.

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:11.720
And I'm not at all ashamed
or embarrassed to speak

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:12.740
of folklore.

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:16.430
Because in a field like optics,
folklore has been, how to say,

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:19.220
taken advantage of a
long, long time ago.

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:22.230
In the fields which I'm
interested in, in fact

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:24.260
very often, very
little was known.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:26.460
Therefore, folklore is
very, very essential,

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:30.290
especially if the folklore
can be, how to say,

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:33.170
changed, modified to
fit the other purposes.

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:36.530
Now, what is this
folklore about primarily?

00:11:36.530 --> 00:11:43.580
Now, this edible vegetable
indicates its structure.

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:45.200
It is called
cauliflower, obviously.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:47.480
Its most striking feature,
as everyone knows,

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:51.129
is that one takes a floret as
opposed to whole cauliflower

00:11:51.129 --> 00:11:52.670
and drops everything
else, the floret

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:55.220
looks exactly like
a small cauliflower.

00:11:55.220 --> 00:11:57.930
And then I can do it
again, again several times.

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:01.730
Well, it's very interesting
that this feature was

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:05.600
known to everyone, but I found
very little mention of it

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:09.042
until my work, perhaps.

00:12:09.042 --> 00:12:10.625
The cauliflowers are
very interesting.

00:12:10.625 --> 00:12:12.930
They look not from
side, but from the top.

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.230
And there are all kinds of
Fibonacci series, all kinds

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:17.120
of structures in them.

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:18.950
These attracted
very much attention.

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:21.500
But I'm not
interested here in how

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:25.640
to say precise description of
the arrangement the florets--

00:12:25.640 --> 00:12:27.390
on something much,
much more important,

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:28.760
which is the roughness.

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:33.250
Certainly, surface of this
vegetable is very rough.

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:36.380
It's very rough in a
very excessive fashion.

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:38.630
That is hierarchical,
the hierarchy,

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:41.450
a big thing made of parts
identical to big thing

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:42.440
and so on and so on.

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:44.600
The parts are well-defined.

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:47.840
Now, the world in general
is not that simple.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:53.360
And in general, this idea of
large and small scale parts

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:56.870
is, of course, all
mixed up and confused.

00:12:56.870 --> 00:12:59.930
Now, this marvelous
graphic is due to my friend

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:03.800
Richard Voss to implement
a model of mountains

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:08.850
that I put forward way
back in the early '70s

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:12.050
before computer was able to
do anything of this sort.

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:15.600
Now, it is not a
picture, photograph.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:17.670
It is not a painting.

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:20.900
It is the implementation
of a mathematical idea.

00:13:20.900 --> 00:13:23.820
And the mathematical idea
is so abstract and so,

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:27.750
how to say, devoid of
substance that I was very much

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:29.520
[? attacked ?] on this account.

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:32.850
The idea is simply that
there is an invariance

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:34.770
between the roughness
of mountains

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:38.530
in big, small, and
very small scales.

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:41.490
Now, again, it was not
something which I, how to say,

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:44.400
quote unquote "invented."

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:46.050
Explorers of the mountains--

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:49.710
Whymper, who climbed the
one Matterhorn, at least

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:52.680
was the first man recorded
as climbing Matterhorn,

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:55.340
wrote about his
rambles in the Alps

00:13:55.340 --> 00:13:58.560
that a small part
of the landscape

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:00.300
is the same as a big part.

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:03.090
And he had all kinds of
philosophical consequences

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:03.780
from it.

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:05.760
But it was certainly
not taken seriously.

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:07.770
So what I did here
was just to embody

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:11.160
this idea of this
wisdom of folklore

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:13.350
and replace by invariance.

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:15.300
Now, one knows the
invariance which

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:18.600
occur in relativity theory,
quantum mechanics, and so on.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:22.090
Here, the invariances are those
of dilation and reduction.

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:25.020
If you dilate it and
reduce it appropriately,

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:26.590
it doesn't change.

00:14:26.590 --> 00:14:28.530
Well, we can go on forever.

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:30.510
But I would like,
first of all, to give

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:33.600
an idea of the nature
of the problem and just

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:36.370
how it's passed.

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:43.770
Fractal is a word
I coined in 1975.

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:46.680
The idea of fractality
did not exist before,

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:49.770
but an intuition of
fractality and, in fact,

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:53.430
examples of use of
fractality go back absolutely

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:54.900
to time immemorial.

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:58.830
This picture here is that
of a village in Tanzania

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:01.700
of a nation which I
don't think lives there.

00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:02.700
It's a ruin.

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:04.830
But if one looks at
this village carefully,

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:06.420
if one looks mostly
from the top which

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:08.710
is less nice photograph
but more telling,

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:11.520
one realizes that's
made entirely

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:12.640
in hierarchical fashion.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:14.100
There's a whole village here.

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:17.790
This thing you see here, which
is the harem of the king, then

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:20.040
the king's house and
so on, everything

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:23.070
is on the same pattern,
but more or less big.

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:25.320
Humanity has always
known of that,

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:27.760
but this was decorative device.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:33.150
And the decorative device was
known, again, to every country.

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:34.230
I chose Africa.

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:37.350
I might have chosen
just as well temples

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:38.880
in many parts of the world.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:42.810
Now, we get to a point,
or two points, rather.

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:46.230
To the right of this picture,
you see the celebrated Face

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:48.240
of War of Dalí.

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:50.090
I don't think Dalí
knew about fractals.

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:52.320
To the left, you
see this [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:53.445
which has a long history.

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:59.490
I first discovered it myself--
"discovered," quote unquote,

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:01.110
"invented," quote unquote.

00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:03.480
And then I realized that
a man named Sierpinski

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:05.564
had written a paper about it.

00:16:05.564 --> 00:16:07.230
And [INAUDIBLE] a
name called Sierpinski

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:09.630
gasket to make it sort of nice.

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:13.080
So it has become a very
important shape in physics.

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:14.520
You see exactly
how it's built. I

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:15.690
don't know [INAUDIBLE]
to tell you.

00:16:15.690 --> 00:16:16.523
You take a triangle.

00:16:16.523 --> 00:16:18.530
You take away small triangles.

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:20.970
Now, the most astounding
fact is that you

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:23.424
go to the Sistine Chapel,
you find approximations of it

00:16:23.424 --> 00:16:24.090
on the pavement.

00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:26.580
If you go to churches, you
find these kind of structures

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:27.360
all the time.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:31.120
It has been all along a
very long decorative design.

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:34.650
So to summarize, fractals have,
on the one hand, a very, very

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:35.880
old history.

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:39.990
And then this
shape, the triangle,

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:42.260
was drawn first around 1900.

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:44.730
Then there was a period
in which mathematicians

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:47.160
started distilling them.

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:50.910
Did they know about the uses
of fractals in decoration

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:51.630
I don't know.

00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:53.500
There is no record
of the telling.

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:57.150
However, Cantor, who was one
of the greatest among them,

00:16:57.150 --> 00:16:59.710
did write the record in
writing of his thinking.

00:16:59.710 --> 00:17:02.190
And he certainly wrote
to his friend [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:06.290
repeatedly that we mathematician
are of a divine race,

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:09.105
and we can devise shapes
that nature does not know.

00:17:09.105 --> 00:17:11.730
So at least some of them thought
they were inventing something.

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:13.109
In fact, they were not.

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:15.900
They were putting in a
very, very precise form

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:19.569
some structures which humanity
had been always familiar with.

00:17:19.569 --> 00:17:25.530
And so then the question arises,
if you want to begin a science,

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:28.470
you certainly must
need numbers for it.

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:35.640
And the astounding finding was
the case at least 15 years ago

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:38.520
that whereas, of
course, loudness

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:40.240
had been measured very well--

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:41.790
a pitch can be
measured very well

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:45.879
by frequency and same thing
for visual impressions.

00:17:45.879 --> 00:17:46.920
Weight has been measured.

00:17:46.920 --> 00:17:48.570
Temperature had been measured.

00:17:48.570 --> 00:17:52.290
That was actual invention
in history by Galileo.

00:17:52.290 --> 00:17:54.960
There was no measurement
of roughness.

00:17:54.960 --> 00:17:58.220
And I wrote in the mid-'80s an
article with some metallurgist

00:17:58.220 --> 00:17:59.580
friend about roughness.

00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:05.130
And he showed me
after many examples

00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:09.311
that in the literature of broken
metals or fractures of metals,

00:18:09.311 --> 00:18:11.310
there are all kinds of
measurements of roughness

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:15.010
which are very, very imperfect
and very, very unsatisfactory.

00:18:15.010 --> 00:18:19.370
Well, so I introduced
in science a notion

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:23.930
which I call fractal dimension,
which takes many shapes.

00:18:23.930 --> 00:18:26.030
I'm going to go very
quickly through it.

00:18:26.030 --> 00:18:30.140
It is a simplification
of something

00:18:30.140 --> 00:18:35.510
which had been used before, used
only in mathematical esoterica

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:36.950
by Hausdorff.

00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:40.440
And that simplification,
that Hausdorff dimension

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:42.930
is impossible in science.

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:47.090
There is no way to measure it,
because operations it embodies.

00:18:47.090 --> 00:18:50.360
The other dimensions
are, indeed, measurable.

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:52.940
And in fact, something
very interesting happened.

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:56.010
You will see here these
dimensions can be fractions.

00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:58.850
And after several
years of working

00:18:58.850 --> 00:19:01.760
with them, in fact doing
nothing else except using them

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:06.140
in one context or another,
I didn't [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:09.410
I taught myself about roughness.

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:11.630
Because everybody, I
think, knows about that.

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:14.240
I taught myself a
correspondence between roughness

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:17.750
and this number.

00:19:17.750 --> 00:19:20.660
An anecdote-- at one time,
a friend came to visit me--

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:22.760
I had then two programmers--

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:25.020
to show us a beautiful
new construction.

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:26.900
And he asked me to
guess dimension.

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:29.150
My two programmers who
had no experience of it,

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:33.500
one said 1.2 and the other said
1.8 just out of their hats.

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:37.040
And I said, it's a
little bit short of 1.5.

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:39.620
It was 1.48.

00:19:39.620 --> 00:19:41.490
And then I'll come
back to an example

00:19:41.490 --> 00:19:43.530
where I guess dimensions
for [? thirds, ?]

00:19:43.530 --> 00:19:45.050
because of being attuned to it.

00:19:45.050 --> 00:19:48.570
Again, I don't think that I
have a skill which is unique.

00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:51.350
I established myself
a correspondence

00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:52.310
between this number.

00:19:52.310 --> 00:19:54.120
Now, how this number come?

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:56.270
It starts with a very,
very trivial property

00:19:56.270 --> 00:19:57.950
of dimension in ordinary cases.

00:19:57.950 --> 00:20:01.730
You divide a string into n
parts or a square handkerchief

00:20:01.730 --> 00:20:03.080
in n parts.

00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:05.240
In each case,
dimension is the ratio

00:20:05.240 --> 00:20:08.630
of log of n divide
by log 1 over r.

00:20:08.630 --> 00:20:10.750
Whenever I publish that
in scientific journal,

00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:13.506
an idiot editor tells
me which [? base. ?]

00:20:13.506 --> 00:20:14.940
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:18.070
Of course, it doesn't
matter, because the ratio.

00:20:18.070 --> 00:20:22.930
Now, instead of putting
the usual shapes,

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:24.880
you take an interval.

00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:27.480
You replace it by the zigzag,
[INAUDIBLE] four parts.

00:20:27.480 --> 00:20:29.340
And you repeat again, again.

00:20:29.340 --> 00:20:34.020
Well, here, if you take
log n over log 1 over r,

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:36.350
you get 1.26, et cetera.

00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:39.050
In the middle part,
you get 1.5 exactly.

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:42.990
In the right part, you get 2.0.

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:45.510
I'm not going to go
into details of it.

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:46.900
It will take forever.

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:49.290
However, these
[INAUDIBLE] quantities

00:20:49.290 --> 00:20:51.540
have been totally
tamed to be measured.

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:56.160
They can be measured sometimes
with exquisite precision.

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:59.610
In one object to which
my current latest

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:04.830
paper is devoted, the
dimension is a 1.71

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:08.280
with some uncertainty
about the next decimal.

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:11.220
It's, well, a little
bit uncertain, not much.

00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:13.620
But it's astounding
that the notion which

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:17.370
is so abstract when properly
generalized and so on becomes

00:21:17.370 --> 00:21:19.450
so completely measurable.

00:21:19.450 --> 00:21:22.440
And so let me now bring
this matter [INAUDIBLE]..

00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:24.600
Now, this picture is in my book.

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:27.349
I forgot which page.

00:21:27.349 --> 00:21:28.890
I was very much in
this Brown motion.

00:21:28.890 --> 00:21:32.910
Brown motion is one of the
center of probability theory.

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:36.210
Of course, it's a
process which was defined

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:41.190
by Norbert Wiener in '20s and
was his greatest claim to fame

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:43.260
for a long time.

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:44.430
It's a drunkard's walk.

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:48.240
And you see this black
line goes around.

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:51.660
So I was drawing these
things all the time.

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:55.190
And in a certain
sense, I was fishing.

00:21:55.190 --> 00:21:56.370
What I mean by fishing?

00:21:56.370 --> 00:21:58.890
Well, I have very
strong impression

00:21:58.890 --> 00:22:02.580
that my colleagues and
friends just underestimate

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:04.560
the power of their eye.

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:06.150
Well, there's
something else to it.

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:08.405
People are more or less
good at it with the eye.

00:22:08.405 --> 00:22:09.800
I mean, it's very clear.

00:22:09.800 --> 00:22:11.190
Scientists are
[INAUDIBLE] mostly

00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:15.390
on exams, which measure
ability to do algebra fast

00:22:15.390 --> 00:22:16.810
and correctly.

00:22:16.810 --> 00:22:18.810
So it's not necessarily
the case that scientists

00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:21.630
have very good eye, at least
not in mathematics and physics.

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:24.300
Certainly, in other
fields, the answer is yes.

00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:28.800
But I do have a great
dependence on my eye.

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:30.720
And I was playing, and
playing, and playing,

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:34.440
and then decided that this
thing was a bit too diffuse.

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:36.600
And the Brownian
motion was [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:36.600 --> 00:22:39.210
here and going around,
around, around, ending here.

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:42.000
So everything I was
looking for was invisible.

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:44.860
Everything I was looking
at had been known before.

00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:46.350
So I decided, first
of all, to have

00:22:46.350 --> 00:22:49.350
the snake bite its tail by
having Brownian motion come

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:51.000
back to where it started.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:52.890
And that's, again,
idea of Norbert Wiener.

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:55.260
It's called a Brownian bridge.

00:22:55.260 --> 00:22:58.600
And then I decided to color it.

00:22:58.600 --> 00:23:00.700
Color doesn't mean I put
fancy colorful things

00:23:00.700 --> 00:23:02.970
like on these mountains
on the Mandelbrot set.

00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:06.400
It's just very simply to make
a difference between inside

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:07.040
and outside.

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:11.380
So all the points which could be
gotten from outside are white.

00:23:11.380 --> 00:23:16.330
All the points which are not
accessible are put in gray.

00:23:16.330 --> 00:23:18.760
When I saw this picture
first on screen,

00:23:18.760 --> 00:23:22.510
I tell you I
[INAUDIBLE] speechless.

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:25.070
This picture screamed at me.

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:26.650
I am an island--

00:23:26.650 --> 00:23:28.370
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:28.370 --> 00:23:30.580
--a very complicated island.

00:23:30.580 --> 00:23:34.850
Now, as Ed has said, the "How
Long the Coast Of Britain?--"

00:23:34.850 --> 00:23:39.110
and you can replace any
island as you wish in it--

00:23:39.110 --> 00:23:40.670
is the very basic issue.

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:44.810
And the length is
quite irrelevant,

00:23:44.810 --> 00:23:46.970
because it depends upon
how you measure it.

00:23:46.970 --> 00:23:49.650
The roughness is
measured by dimension.

00:23:49.650 --> 00:23:56.270
And so I've seen many islands,
real ones and fake ones.

00:23:56.270 --> 00:24:00.150
And this one was
very, very irregular.

00:24:00.150 --> 00:24:05.900
So my first reaction was about
the most difficult islands I've

00:24:05.900 --> 00:24:06.876
seen were for 4/3.

00:24:06.876 --> 00:24:07.820
1.5, it goes beyond.

00:24:07.820 --> 00:24:10.940
4/3 was the kind
of magic number.

00:24:10.940 --> 00:24:15.390
4/3 was the islands I was
very much pleased with

00:24:15.390 --> 00:24:18.240
as being sort of irregular.

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:20.150
They measured it, because
man can measure it.

00:24:20.150 --> 00:24:26.510
And the answer was that
the dimension was 1.33336.

00:24:26.510 --> 00:24:30.420
Well, this is a
humongous simulation,

00:24:30.420 --> 00:24:32.390
so you can get these
things very accurately.

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:34.120
It was 4/3.

00:24:34.120 --> 00:24:36.500
Now, the most extraordinary
thing about that

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:38.960
is that, if you begin
to be attuned to it,

00:24:38.960 --> 00:24:41.600
you look in these
kind of contours

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:42.770
for all kinds of shapes.

00:24:42.770 --> 00:24:45.290
And some people claim
that the left part of it

00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:48.920
is Spain, which had been sort
of made bigger, that England,

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:52.040
of course, had been vanished
[INAUDIBLE] different island.

00:24:52.040 --> 00:24:54.810
Scandinavia was there
a bit out of whack.

00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:58.040
Anyhow, people begin to see
actual geographical features

00:24:58.040 --> 00:24:59.820
very easily.

00:24:59.820 --> 00:25:01.810
And that's sort of
almost automatic.

00:25:01.810 --> 00:25:05.120
You do curves of this
dimension, and you find it.

00:25:05.120 --> 00:25:12.020
Well, 4/3 was for a long time
a conjecture for 80 years.

00:25:12.020 --> 00:25:15.380
Then a friend of mine,
[? Bertoli Brontier, ?]

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:18.380
gave a demonstration
of the 4/3 which

00:25:18.380 --> 00:25:21.690
was very beautiful,
but not rigorous.

00:25:21.690 --> 00:25:24.194
He calls that exact,
but not rigorous.

00:25:24.194 --> 00:25:25.860
It uses alternate
techniques of physics,

00:25:25.860 --> 00:25:29.090
which are probably correct,
but are not certified as such.

00:25:29.090 --> 00:25:35.350
And then shortly afterwards,
Schramm, Lawler, and Werner

00:25:35.350 --> 00:25:36.140
proved it.

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:40.220
Now, what is
interesting here is not

00:25:40.220 --> 00:25:41.550
that the theorem was proven.

00:25:41.550 --> 00:25:43.550
Actually, the proof takes
several hundred pages.

00:25:43.550 --> 00:25:46.330
It's extremely difficult,
techniques of every kind.

00:25:46.330 --> 00:25:48.110
It's some overwhelming thing.

00:25:48.110 --> 00:25:51.350
The interesting thing was
the extraordinary short path

00:25:51.350 --> 00:25:55.830
between a childishly simple
thing, namely a drunkard going

00:25:55.830 --> 00:26:01.430
around, and then a question
which is very difficult, which

00:26:01.430 --> 00:26:05.780
for 20 years baffled the most
brilliant among my friends

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:07.250
and was topic of great despair.

00:26:07.250 --> 00:26:08.666
First of all, they
said, I'm going

00:26:08.666 --> 00:26:12.090
to do it overnight, then next
week, next month, next year.

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:13.340
Finally, it took a millennium.

00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:15.160
Well, it didn't
take a millennium,

00:26:15.160 --> 00:26:17.990
but it had to wait
until next millennium.

00:26:17.990 --> 00:26:19.950
It's very often the case.

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:22.850
And I think that actually
it is representative

00:26:22.850 --> 00:26:28.502
of a feature what I say about
roughness, that roughness

00:26:28.502 --> 00:26:32.880
have not been explored
systematically for itself.

00:26:32.880 --> 00:26:36.290
I mean, I made a lifetime
devotion to it, but was not.

00:26:36.290 --> 00:26:38.930
Therefore, it is not a case
in which simple problems have

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:41.990
been solved long ago and the
only difficult ones remain.

00:26:41.990 --> 00:26:43.700
Simple to state, I mean.

00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:46.310
It is also a field
which is not very old.

00:26:46.310 --> 00:26:48.920
So there are very few cases
in which a problem arose out

00:26:48.920 --> 00:26:50.210
of previous problem.

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:52.460
Problems, mathematical
questions arise out

00:26:52.460 --> 00:26:54.920
of things which are
very, very common.

00:26:54.920 --> 00:26:56.450
And I will tell
you at the end more

00:26:56.450 --> 00:26:59.342
about my very gay
commitment now to education.

00:26:59.342 --> 00:27:00.800
And one of the
aspects of education

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:06.140
is to realize that even
adolescents, sometimes

00:27:06.140 --> 00:27:08.780
your small children,
junior high and so on,

00:27:08.780 --> 00:27:11.870
are very comfortable with
the concepts that describe

00:27:11.870 --> 00:27:13.710
roughness, the concept
of self-similarity,

00:27:13.710 --> 00:27:15.380
the concept of algorithm.

00:27:15.380 --> 00:27:17.970
I mean, if no
obfuscation is added,

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:19.490
they understand very well.

00:27:19.490 --> 00:27:24.440
And then when they see that
by a little manipulation

00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:25.980
from these very,
very simple things,

00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:27.930
one gets these very
complicated matters,

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:31.160
I think that we make them
understand to at least

00:27:31.160 --> 00:27:35.630
some extent this notion of
extraordinary fruitfulness

00:27:35.630 --> 00:27:40.560
of science in creating
problems as it develops.

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:43.610
And, well, it's a
course at Yale, which

00:27:43.610 --> 00:27:49.150
I expressed a very [INAUDIBLE].

00:27:49.150 --> 00:27:50.640
I will tell you more about that.

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:55.030
It said the last line,
"token of the value

00:27:55.030 --> 00:27:58.930
of the eye's recent return
into pure mathematics."

00:27:58.930 --> 00:28:00.097
Expression of that is that--

00:28:00.097 --> 00:28:02.221
there's an article about
that in the French edition

00:28:02.221 --> 00:28:03.250
of Scientific American.

00:28:03.250 --> 00:28:07.300
And the illustrator
felt obliged to mark

00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:09.340
the boundary of this object.

00:28:09.340 --> 00:28:11.110
The whole point--
that the boundary

00:28:11.110 --> 00:28:12.610
didn't have to be marked.

00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:15.340
The boundary was visible
without being marked.

00:28:15.340 --> 00:28:16.900
You didn't have to mark it.

00:28:16.900 --> 00:28:23.620
Well, all that cries
out for simpler proofs.

00:28:23.620 --> 00:28:27.140
And I'm going to go further.

00:28:27.140 --> 00:28:29.170
The critical application
clusters I would not

00:28:29.170 --> 00:28:32.950
go into details of what it is.

00:28:32.950 --> 00:28:35.710
But the general idea
is very close to what

00:28:35.710 --> 00:28:37.440
the structure of
magnet [INAUDIBLE]..

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:40.230
A magnet is made of little
magnets going up or down.

00:28:40.230 --> 00:28:44.470
And our critical percolation
clusters complicate shapes.

00:28:44.470 --> 00:28:47.440
And they, too, have boundaries
of dimensions, 4/3 and also

00:28:47.440 --> 00:28:49.870
7/4, because depending
what you look at,

00:28:49.870 --> 00:28:52.270
you get either 4/3 or 7/4.

00:28:52.270 --> 00:28:54.060
It was the object of
great fascination.

00:28:54.060 --> 00:28:57.490
[INAUDIBLE] measured
after I [INAUDIBLE] 4/3.

00:28:57.490 --> 00:29:01.420
And, again, [INAUDIBLE]
did something about it.

00:29:01.420 --> 00:29:05.980
And [? Stan Smirnov ?]
proved it earlier this year.

00:29:05.980 --> 00:29:08.170
It's a whole field
which has arisen

00:29:08.170 --> 00:29:10.660
which now is completely
independent of the eye,

00:29:10.660 --> 00:29:13.720
of course, but which would not
have arisen without the eye.

00:29:13.720 --> 00:29:16.870
And I really think that it's
very important from a viewpoint

00:29:16.870 --> 00:29:22.360
of an understanding of science
to realize what has happened.

00:29:22.360 --> 00:29:27.040
The reason why the eye went
out of the hard sciences

00:29:27.040 --> 00:29:30.950
was not a, how do you say,
decision by some committee

00:29:30.950 --> 00:29:31.750
to neglect it.

00:29:31.750 --> 00:29:35.200
It was observation
that, as time went on,

00:29:35.200 --> 00:29:38.080
new questions did not
come from the eye.

00:29:38.080 --> 00:29:40.330
New questions came
from old questions.

00:29:40.330 --> 00:29:43.660
Also, the fact that once those
sciences were established,

00:29:43.660 --> 00:29:46.390
again, the simplest things
were very rapidly done.

00:29:46.390 --> 00:29:48.010
And then the more
complicated things

00:29:48.010 --> 00:29:50.890
came from simpler things
in the field itself.

00:29:50.890 --> 00:29:55.420
But in the case of the
prices of roughness

00:29:55.420 --> 00:29:58.444
and of the topics I've
discussed all my life,

00:29:58.444 --> 00:29:59.610
situation is very different.

00:29:59.610 --> 00:30:03.880
It's still very, very close
to the initial basic issues.

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:08.730
And therefore, the eye had
been absolutely indispensable,

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:11.810
the eye and sometimes
even hearing.

00:30:11.810 --> 00:30:14.890
In many cases, I have
transformed some signals

00:30:14.890 --> 00:30:20.080
into noises which are audible
and discovered, in so doing,

00:30:20.080 --> 00:30:23.110
that phenomena which
previous analysis had

00:30:23.110 --> 00:30:24.522
declared to be identical.

00:30:24.522 --> 00:30:26.230
They were, in fact,
very, very different.

00:30:26.230 --> 00:30:29.500
They sound very different.

00:30:29.500 --> 00:30:34.139
Now, I would like to just
make one rapid step back

00:30:34.139 --> 00:30:35.680
to the Mandelbrot
set, which I didn't

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:40.300
want to spend all my time
on, in fact almost no time.

00:30:40.300 --> 00:30:43.360
When I became fascinated
by the subject,

00:30:43.360 --> 00:30:48.490
discovered it and described
it around '79, '80,

00:30:48.490 --> 00:30:50.080
I started with a
definition which

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:53.080
is different from the one
which everybody now uses.

00:30:53.080 --> 00:30:56.550
And it turned out that
it's very difficult

00:30:56.550 --> 00:30:59.140
to do numerically
on the computer

00:30:59.140 --> 00:31:01.720
at a time, very clumsy.

00:31:01.720 --> 00:31:04.120
And so I changed definition,
which is a process

00:31:04.120 --> 00:31:04.990
mathematicians like.

00:31:04.990 --> 00:31:07.000
But in this case, I
changed definitions

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:09.280
thinking the two
definitions were identical.

00:31:09.280 --> 00:31:11.910
And the second definition
is one everybody knows.

00:31:11.910 --> 00:31:13.780
It's very easy to program.

00:31:13.780 --> 00:31:19.140
And all these incredible
riches came out pouring.

00:31:19.140 --> 00:31:21.970
And observations
made on the pictures

00:31:21.970 --> 00:31:23.590
became mathematical conjectures.

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:27.250
And they're modified,
proven with great success.

00:31:27.250 --> 00:31:30.860
But the initial idea that the
two definitions are identical,

00:31:30.860 --> 00:31:35.620
where one set is the closure
of the other, that assumption,

00:31:35.620 --> 00:31:39.430
conjecture, whatever,
is still not proven.

00:31:39.430 --> 00:31:44.910
We use that and Brownian
cluster in this course at Yale

00:31:44.910 --> 00:31:47.890
on fractals for
non-mathematician

00:31:47.890 --> 00:31:51.670
and also in workshops
for high school teachers,

00:31:51.670 --> 00:31:55.140
because there is
nothing there which

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:57.070
has to be left unexplained.

00:31:57.070 --> 00:32:00.010
Everything is there will
explain in great detail.

00:32:00.010 --> 00:32:02.170
And they understand
very well the nature

00:32:02.170 --> 00:32:04.210
of some problems, which
otherwise would not

00:32:04.210 --> 00:32:06.250
be understood.

00:32:06.250 --> 00:32:10.330
Now, let me change topic
substantially, but not so

00:32:10.330 --> 00:32:11.200
brutally.

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:14.890
Because what I'm going to say
applies not only to prices,

00:32:14.890 --> 00:32:17.020
but also to many
noises, many phenomena

00:32:17.020 --> 00:32:20.150
in nature in many fields.

00:32:20.150 --> 00:32:24.570
But this corresponds to one
of my earliest fascinations

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:31.220
with financial prices in the
early '60s and then a topic

00:32:31.220 --> 00:32:34.490
which I very much devote
a very big part of my life

00:32:34.490 --> 00:32:36.740
to at present.

00:32:36.740 --> 00:32:40.090
Now, prices, of
course, go up and down.

00:32:40.090 --> 00:32:41.480
That was known to everybody.

00:32:41.480 --> 00:32:44.150
And there are all kinds
of nice maxims about it.

00:32:44.150 --> 00:32:49.130
In 1900, an incredible
genius looked at a problem.

00:32:49.130 --> 00:32:51.350
His name was Louis Bachelier.

00:32:51.350 --> 00:32:52.720
Nobody noticed him.

00:32:52.720 --> 00:32:54.350
He had a very miserable life.

00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:58.550
But he wrote in 1900 a
PhD thesis in mathematics,

00:32:58.550 --> 00:33:01.130
believe it or not, called
the Theory of Speculation.

00:33:01.130 --> 00:33:04.400
Speculation meant speculation
on the stock market or bond

00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:05.630
market.

00:33:05.630 --> 00:33:07.640
And he introduced
for the first time,

00:33:07.640 --> 00:33:12.500
in loose and incomplete
fashion, the Brownian motion

00:33:12.500 --> 00:33:18.590
which Wiener later made into
a central mathematical topic

00:33:18.590 --> 00:33:21.780
and which [INAUDIBLE]
Einstein and others made

00:33:21.780 --> 00:33:23.549
the central physical topic.

00:33:23.549 --> 00:33:25.340
Now, the idea of
Bachelier was more or less

00:33:25.340 --> 00:33:27.590
that prices vary at random.

00:33:27.590 --> 00:33:28.880
You can't predict them.

00:33:28.880 --> 00:33:30.030
You toss a coin.

00:33:30.030 --> 00:33:31.490
If it's heads, your price go up.

00:33:31.490 --> 00:33:33.560
It's tails, price goes down.

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:35.580
And you go on and on and on.

00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:38.180
Well, you can approximate
that by sequence

00:33:38.180 --> 00:33:39.620
of independent
Gaussian variables.

00:33:39.620 --> 00:33:42.140
And you see it on
top very clearly.

00:33:42.140 --> 00:33:45.090
The sequence of independent
Gaussian variables,

00:33:45.090 --> 00:33:49.630
which are the increments
of Brownian motion, it's

00:33:49.630 --> 00:33:51.600
called also white
noise, carelessly

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:53.210
called white noise,
which we call

00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:55.190
Gaussian, Gaussian white noise.

00:33:55.190 --> 00:33:58.950
It makes a very big difference
I am going to argue in a second.

00:33:58.950 --> 00:34:03.920
Now, much later, a whole
theory of stock market

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:07.910
occurred on the basis that
this model Bachelier is indeed

00:34:07.910 --> 00:34:09.889
a representation of reality.

00:34:09.889 --> 00:34:15.679
And the size of these
increments of price, this size

00:34:15.679 --> 00:34:17.480
is what's called volatility.

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:19.719
The model assumes
constant volatility.

00:34:19.719 --> 00:34:23.510
And indeed, if once you've seen
a little piece of this line

00:34:23.510 --> 00:34:26.360
on top, you have seen them all.

00:34:26.360 --> 00:34:28.080
They're all very much alike.

00:34:28.080 --> 00:34:31.400
And if you average it, if you
take averages, moving averages,

00:34:31.400 --> 00:34:35.409
this whole thing goes to
zero very, very quickly.

00:34:35.409 --> 00:34:39.239
Now, look immediately at
the bottom five lines.

00:34:39.239 --> 00:34:41.780
Skip two, I'll come
back to them later.

00:34:41.780 --> 00:34:44.600
At least one of them is a real
price series, price changes.

00:34:44.600 --> 00:34:47.510
Or at least one of
them is an illustration

00:34:47.510 --> 00:34:50.929
of my current forgery, which is
called the multi-fractal model

00:34:50.929 --> 00:34:52.880
of price variation.

00:34:52.880 --> 00:34:56.090
Now, I'm sure that
many, many people here

00:34:56.090 --> 00:34:58.850
are such great geniuses
that you guess immediately

00:34:58.850 --> 00:35:01.670
which is which, but
you are exceptional.

00:35:01.670 --> 00:35:03.170
Most people can't do it.

00:35:03.170 --> 00:35:07.250
Because all these phenomena have
a very strange characteristic.

00:35:07.250 --> 00:35:09.470
They have very big
peaks all the time.

00:35:09.470 --> 00:35:11.670
And the peaks don't
arise by themselves.

00:35:11.670 --> 00:35:15.494
They arise in the middle of
periods of great volatility,

00:35:15.494 --> 00:35:17.660
then there are periods of
very, very low volatility.

00:35:17.660 --> 00:35:20.720
There are periods where
volatility suddenly changes.

00:35:20.720 --> 00:35:26.120
Volatility, as you try to grab
it in these sequences, either

00:35:26.120 --> 00:35:29.730
the fake or the real ones,
is something very, very, very

00:35:29.730 --> 00:35:30.230
elusive.

00:35:30.230 --> 00:35:35.460
In fact, it's
impossible to grasp.

00:35:35.460 --> 00:35:38.870
And so how are we
going to be represent

00:35:38.870 --> 00:35:40.790
that [INAUDIBLE] phenomena?

00:35:40.790 --> 00:35:47.370
And I put this
example in particular,

00:35:47.370 --> 00:35:50.220
because it puts
in contrast very,

00:35:50.220 --> 00:35:54.960
very sharply the
approach to roughness

00:35:54.960 --> 00:35:59.430
which has been used by,
I think, most authors

00:35:59.430 --> 00:36:02.040
before fractals and the
approach to roughness

00:36:02.040 --> 00:36:03.512
which I've been advocating.

00:36:03.512 --> 00:36:05.220
And I would like to
emphasize differences

00:36:05.220 --> 00:36:08.915
without going into
long calculations.

00:36:08.915 --> 00:36:11.040
The approach which more
people have been advocating

00:36:11.040 --> 00:36:14.780
is, if a constant volatility
phenomenon doesn't work,

00:36:14.780 --> 00:36:19.832
then replace it by something
which is a variable volatility.

00:36:19.832 --> 00:36:21.540
And if that doesn't
work, then replace it

00:36:21.540 --> 00:36:26.560
by something at
discontinuities or jumps.

00:36:26.560 --> 00:36:28.240
Well, this is very
much the spirit

00:36:28.240 --> 00:36:36.170
of the celebrated model
of the motion of planets

00:36:36.170 --> 00:36:37.530
due to Ptolemaeus.

00:36:37.530 --> 00:36:41.140
In the Ptolemaic
model, the planets

00:36:41.140 --> 00:36:46.600
go around circles and then
there are epicycles, which are--

00:36:46.600 --> 00:36:48.310
and then the
epicycles, planet is

00:36:48.310 --> 00:36:51.610
going on circles, which center
of which goes another circle.

00:36:51.610 --> 00:36:53.980
And then in addition,
we must assume

00:36:53.980 --> 00:36:56.800
that the center of
the basic circle

00:36:56.800 --> 00:37:00.940
is not identical to the
center of the universe.

00:37:00.940 --> 00:37:03.250
In fact, there
are three factors.

00:37:03.250 --> 00:37:06.310
Everybody knows how
Kepler replaced that

00:37:06.310 --> 00:37:09.540
by a different formula which
later Newton transformed

00:37:09.540 --> 00:37:11.920
by explaining it
through gravitation.

00:37:11.920 --> 00:37:13.850
But the main fact
is that there are

00:37:13.850 --> 00:37:16.750
two approaches to
this phenomenon,

00:37:16.750 --> 00:37:20.260
either that the prices
which are in the lower part

00:37:20.260 --> 00:37:23.400
are just other
examples of something

00:37:23.400 --> 00:37:27.160
complicated, unpleasant
which are just combinations

00:37:27.160 --> 00:37:30.580
of the simple, or that there is
something radically new which

00:37:30.580 --> 00:37:32.030
is involved.

00:37:32.030 --> 00:37:34.600
And I'll tell you
about how radically new

00:37:34.600 --> 00:37:36.370
this thing has to be.

00:37:36.370 --> 00:37:40.390
Many of these very big peaks
we see on the five lower lines

00:37:40.390 --> 00:37:44.649
exceed, well, something which is
defined as standard deviation.

00:37:44.649 --> 00:37:46.690
Actually, I don't know
how to exactly measure it,

00:37:46.690 --> 00:37:51.970
but sort of 10 times bigger than
the standard deviation of most

00:37:51.970 --> 00:37:54.830
examples, the quote
"10-sigma events."

00:37:54.830 --> 00:37:57.100
Now, 10-sigma events in
the Gaussian process,

00:37:57.100 --> 00:37:59.200
Gaussian independent
process on top,

00:37:59.200 --> 00:38:01.420
the probability would be
one millionth, a millionth,

00:38:01.420 --> 00:38:03.130
a millionth, a millionth.

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:07.790
The inverse of Avogadro' number,
it's a very small number.

00:38:07.790 --> 00:38:11.560
If that were the case, these
things will never happen.

00:38:11.560 --> 00:38:14.420
But as you see here, they
happened all the time.

00:38:14.420 --> 00:38:17.350
So it's not a matter of adding
a little bit of large values,

00:38:17.350 --> 00:38:21.070
because one gets in a situation
in which the large values

00:38:21.070 --> 00:38:24.400
dominate everything
very strongly.

00:38:24.400 --> 00:38:27.190
As matter of fact, I'm going
to go a bit illogically

00:38:27.190 --> 00:38:30.430
to make a point early on.

00:38:30.430 --> 00:38:34.030
The central aspect of
statistical physics,

00:38:34.030 --> 00:38:38.440
statistical thermodynamics,
is that a big system evolves

00:38:38.440 --> 00:38:42.510
in a certain way, namely entropy
increases with probability one.

00:38:42.510 --> 00:38:45.770
With probability zero,
entropy is going to decrease.

00:38:45.770 --> 00:38:51.440
But the events of probability
zero are totally negligible.

00:38:51.440 --> 00:38:55.000
The events which count are
those of probability one.

00:38:55.000 --> 00:38:57.460
Average, of course, converge.

00:38:57.460 --> 00:38:59.140
The past and the
future are independent.

00:38:59.140 --> 00:39:01.760
All kinds of beautiful
theorems apply.

00:39:01.760 --> 00:39:07.560
But if you go to
financial prices,

00:39:07.560 --> 00:39:09.550
it's, again, part of folklore.

00:39:09.550 --> 00:39:13.240
If you look at last
10 years and wonder,

00:39:13.240 --> 00:39:16.480
assuming that somebody
with miraculous powers

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:20.230
of seeing in the dark some
devil would pick the days

00:39:20.230 --> 00:39:23.890
that matter, you realize
that the days that matter

00:39:23.890 --> 00:39:26.860
were just 10 days
out of last 10 years.

00:39:26.860 --> 00:39:28.150
Very few days matter.

00:39:28.150 --> 00:39:31.150
And the great fortunes
were made in very few days.

00:39:31.150 --> 00:39:34.420
Great ruins happened
very few days.

00:39:34.420 --> 00:39:36.590
So one gets ultimately
the situation

00:39:36.590 --> 00:39:39.280
which is very, very unsettling.

00:39:39.280 --> 00:39:43.840
That is, in this
context, already very

00:39:43.840 --> 00:39:46.540
few rare events
count overwhelmingly.

00:39:46.540 --> 00:39:51.490
And the rest doesn't
count hardly at all.

00:39:51.490 --> 00:39:59.350
It's a context in which one
must review one's intuition very

00:39:59.350 --> 00:40:00.340
strongly.

00:40:00.340 --> 00:40:03.610
But reviewing and
reviewing, it's

00:40:03.610 --> 00:40:09.340
best not to abandon
[INAUDIBLE] and abandon

00:40:09.340 --> 00:40:11.020
the primary principle
of modeling,

00:40:11.020 --> 00:40:14.870
which is that of invariance.

00:40:14.870 --> 00:40:16.200
It becomes almost a joke--

00:40:16.200 --> 00:40:17.520
I said to you before--

00:40:17.520 --> 00:40:20.700
to others, to what extent some
scientists, I am one of them,

00:40:20.700 --> 00:40:23.940
emphasizes that basic trick
of science is invariance.

00:40:23.940 --> 00:40:26.230
You find the right invariance.

00:40:26.230 --> 00:40:28.650
And in fundamental physics,
you look for all kinds

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:30.000
of new invariances.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:33.270
And the lucky ones or
the [INAUDIBLE] ones

00:40:33.270 --> 00:40:35.340
are the ones who
find better theories.

00:40:35.340 --> 00:40:38.700
In this case, the matter was
to find the right invariance.

00:40:38.700 --> 00:40:41.630
And so I'm giving you the key.

00:40:41.630 --> 00:40:46.230
The forgeries which I
introduced in among these lines

00:40:46.230 --> 00:40:49.589
were not chosen by
taking the line on top

00:40:49.589 --> 00:40:51.630
and adding this, adding
that, adding this, adding

00:40:51.630 --> 00:40:55.260
that until the thing looked
more or less reasonable.

00:40:55.260 --> 00:40:58.160
It can be done, but I
don't do it this way.

00:40:58.160 --> 00:41:00.660
The key is to find
the right invariance.

00:41:00.660 --> 00:41:05.220
And lo and behold, the right
invariance gives this result.

00:41:05.220 --> 00:41:08.370
Now, let me again
take a step back,

00:41:08.370 --> 00:41:11.460
because it's a lesson
which not only applies

00:41:11.460 --> 00:41:15.300
to this, financial prices,
but too many other phenomena.

00:41:15.300 --> 00:41:19.860
Believe it or not, but
the five bottom lines

00:41:19.860 --> 00:41:22.500
are all white noises.

00:41:22.500 --> 00:41:23.640
I repeat.

00:41:23.640 --> 00:41:26.680
They have a spectrum,
which is constant.

00:41:26.680 --> 00:41:28.860
There's no correlation.

00:41:28.860 --> 00:41:32.160
But [INAUDIBLE] it's full of
very interesting structures.

00:41:32.160 --> 00:41:35.610
How can a white noise
be so interesting?

00:41:35.610 --> 00:41:38.380
I'm used to white noise like
on top, which is boring,

00:41:38.380 --> 00:41:41.850
which is like you've seen one
Redwood you've seen them all,

00:41:41.850 --> 00:41:44.775
like former President
Reagan said.

00:41:44.775 --> 00:41:48.000
At the bottom,
everything is different.

00:41:48.000 --> 00:41:50.490
Well, the fact is that the
bottom five lines are not

00:41:50.490 --> 00:41:51.600
Gaussian.

00:41:51.600 --> 00:41:54.680
Therefore, if not
Gaussian, whiteness

00:41:54.680 --> 00:41:56.430
does not imply boredom.

00:41:56.430 --> 00:41:59.790
Whiteness implies, very
often, a very rough ride.

00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:08.890
So a tool of science,
namely the spectrum,

00:42:08.890 --> 00:42:12.450
which had been marvelous is
not applicable in this context.

00:42:12.450 --> 00:42:13.200
It doesn't work.

00:42:13.200 --> 00:42:17.250
I mean, again, Wiener was
very clear when he described

00:42:17.250 --> 00:42:19.860
spectral analysis early on--

00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:24.460
as you realize, Wiener was one
of my intellectual heroes--

00:42:24.460 --> 00:42:27.990
when he described the condition
which spectra are good.

00:42:27.990 --> 00:42:31.710
But these conditions that are
not satisfied for these data.

00:42:31.710 --> 00:42:34.620
Now, is that a new finding
by [INAUDIBLE] Mandelbrot

00:42:34.620 --> 00:42:36.570
that these are white?

00:42:36.570 --> 00:42:38.190
By no means.

00:42:38.190 --> 00:42:43.660
I remember when spectrum became
usable after the Fast Fourier

00:42:43.660 --> 00:42:48.160
Transform was discovered,
rediscovered-- rediscovered,

00:42:48.160 --> 00:42:49.500
actually.

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:52.920
Everybody went on to spectral
analyze everything in sight.

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:55.910
And prices were
spectral analyzed.

00:42:55.910 --> 00:42:58.260
They turned out to be
white, price changes.

00:42:58.260 --> 00:43:03.100
And the result was
absolute, absolute horror.

00:43:03.100 --> 00:43:04.440
How can it be?

00:43:04.440 --> 00:43:07.130
If it's white, it should
be like line on top.

00:43:07.130 --> 00:43:09.070
Now, every book of
mathematics says

00:43:09.070 --> 00:43:12.760
that independence and
orthogonality are the same

00:43:12.760 --> 00:43:15.320
for Gaussian processes.

00:43:15.320 --> 00:43:19.060
Orthogonality means a white
spectrum in this case.

00:43:19.060 --> 00:43:21.370
But nobody paid attention
to it, because the examples

00:43:21.370 --> 00:43:25.635
given when they are different
are very made up examples.

00:43:25.635 --> 00:43:26.995
These are not made up examples.

00:43:26.995 --> 00:43:29.930
They are examples
made by culture

00:43:29.930 --> 00:43:33.370
in so far as stock market is
not part of nature, therefore

00:43:33.370 --> 00:43:39.270
it's part of culture, or made
by me which is also cultural.

00:43:39.270 --> 00:43:41.950
But so you can have
structures in this fashion.

00:43:41.950 --> 00:43:45.040
Now, the other
thing is the habit

00:43:45.040 --> 00:43:48.640
came from thermodynamics
that, if you

00:43:48.640 --> 00:43:52.550
take a big enough system, then
fluctuation does not matter.

00:43:52.550 --> 00:43:55.090
In fact, the whole thing
was proving larger, bigger

00:43:55.090 --> 00:43:56.410
and bigger system.

00:43:56.410 --> 00:44:02.860
Ultimately, fluctuations don't
matter, and they average out.

00:44:02.860 --> 00:44:06.199
But look at this
thing on the left.

00:44:06.199 --> 00:44:07.490
It is not quite the same thing.

00:44:07.490 --> 00:44:09.340
On the right, it's
squares of it.

00:44:09.340 --> 00:44:10.840
And there's some
hanky-panky to it,

00:44:10.840 --> 00:44:12.780
but it's about,
roughly speaking,

00:44:12.780 --> 00:44:15.340
variation of prices
over a century.

00:44:15.340 --> 00:44:18.400
If these little
things right were just

00:44:18.400 --> 00:44:23.300
fleeting defects which average
out, then over a century

00:44:23.300 --> 00:44:26.260
they've been very, very smooth
indeed and certainly not smooth

00:44:26.260 --> 00:44:27.250
at all.

00:44:27.250 --> 00:44:30.460
So we did a phenomena in which
averaging does not really

00:44:30.460 --> 00:44:32.290
make any simpler.

00:44:32.290 --> 00:44:37.180
And that's, again, the signature
of an invariant processes,

00:44:37.180 --> 00:44:39.470
the same as small
scale, big scale.

00:44:39.470 --> 00:44:43.210
Self-similarity or variance
thereof enter into the game.

00:44:46.870 --> 00:44:49.690
So the models I'm going
to need to say a few more

00:44:49.690 --> 00:44:52.000
words about that
multi-fractal models.

00:44:52.000 --> 00:44:56.250
But the second line on top
was my first model of prices.

00:44:56.250 --> 00:44:58.000
I call it mesofractal.

00:44:58.000 --> 00:44:59.920
Now, it's a new
term, because I need

00:44:59.920 --> 00:45:01.840
these terms for various reason.

00:45:01.840 --> 00:45:05.350
At that time, I was so
focused on large values

00:45:05.350 --> 00:45:09.100
that I neglected the
dependence between prices.

00:45:09.100 --> 00:45:11.350
The bottom lines
show you the prices

00:45:11.350 --> 00:45:12.430
have enormous dependence.

00:45:12.430 --> 00:45:14.590
I mean, that's why people can--

00:45:14.590 --> 00:45:17.680
there's a [INAUDIBLE] of
prediction actually possible--

00:45:17.680 --> 00:45:21.910
well, in one form or
another, but that's

00:45:21.910 --> 00:45:23.350
a different issue here.

00:45:23.350 --> 00:45:27.180
But the second line
was independence,

00:45:27.180 --> 00:45:28.862
had these very long peaks.

00:45:28.862 --> 00:45:30.820
And that took care of
one of the problems which

00:45:30.820 --> 00:45:34.360
is the long term dependence, the
long tails, the large values.

00:45:34.360 --> 00:45:35.510
Then I had another way.

00:45:35.510 --> 00:45:37.870
The second line took care
of long-term dependence.

00:45:37.870 --> 00:45:41.151
But only multi-fractals
took care of both.

00:45:41.151 --> 00:45:42.650
It's interesting
that multi-fractals

00:45:42.650 --> 00:45:46.269
and multi-fractality is a
notion which I introduced about

00:45:46.269 --> 00:45:48.060
the time I met Ed
Lorenz, a matter of fact,

00:45:48.060 --> 00:45:52.840
a little bit later to explain
the nature of dissipation

00:45:52.840 --> 00:45:53.962
of turbulence--

00:45:53.962 --> 00:45:54.920
not explain, I'm sorry.

00:45:54.920 --> 00:45:56.110
I take it back--

00:45:56.110 --> 00:45:57.910
describe it.

00:45:57.910 --> 00:46:00.820
Explain is a big word.

00:46:00.820 --> 00:46:03.640
Describe, because
it's a topic in which

00:46:03.640 --> 00:46:07.900
truly the natural tools borrowed
from other successful sciences

00:46:07.900 --> 00:46:09.820
have been quite unsuccessful.

00:46:09.820 --> 00:46:11.870
Now, let me explain
to you how here

00:46:11.870 --> 00:46:15.460
this matter of
self-sameness which

00:46:15.460 --> 00:46:18.210
is no longer self-similarity,
but self-affinity,

00:46:18.210 --> 00:46:20.260
entered [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:20.260 --> 00:46:22.720
So this is not a
model of price change.

00:46:22.720 --> 00:46:24.040
It's a cartoon.

00:46:24.040 --> 00:46:24.830
What's a cartoon?

00:46:24.830 --> 00:46:29.650
A cartoon is something which
has recognizably same features,

00:46:29.650 --> 00:46:32.710
but which is simpler
to draw and to behold.

00:46:32.710 --> 00:46:36.280
So on top, you have
a straight line trend

00:46:36.280 --> 00:46:38.980
which the price changed
from beginning to end.

00:46:38.980 --> 00:46:42.180
I was told, by all means,
it must be going up.

00:46:42.180 --> 00:46:43.510
So it's going up.

00:46:43.510 --> 00:46:46.659
And then there is this
variation of this price,

00:46:46.659 --> 00:46:48.700
which is the second line,
which I call generator.

00:46:48.700 --> 00:46:50.530
It goes up, up, down, up.

00:46:50.530 --> 00:46:53.600
And to simplify it,
I make it symmetric.

00:46:53.600 --> 00:46:55.760
Then for each of the
next stages, what I do

00:46:55.760 --> 00:46:58.000
is we take this
generator and squeeze it.

00:46:58.000 --> 00:47:00.610
But, you see, you must squeeze
it in different ratios this way

00:47:00.610 --> 00:47:01.990
and that way.

00:47:01.990 --> 00:47:04.510
If the ratio of squeezing
were the same both directions,

00:47:04.510 --> 00:47:06.405
you would deal with similarity.

00:47:06.405 --> 00:47:08.155
And the [INAUDIBLE]
is self-similar.

00:47:08.155 --> 00:47:11.080
We squeeze the different
rates this way and this way.

00:47:11.080 --> 00:47:12.760
It is an affinity
mathematically.

00:47:12.760 --> 00:47:15.310
Therefore, these
things are self-affine.

00:47:15.310 --> 00:47:17.390
And if it goes down,
you turn the thing back.

00:47:17.390 --> 00:47:18.970
You do it again, again.

00:47:18.970 --> 00:47:23.090
Now, after a while, you
get this jagged curve,

00:47:23.090 --> 00:47:25.910
which has a certain amount
of boring regularity.

00:47:25.910 --> 00:47:29.480
So you inject the minimum
amount of randomness into this.

00:47:29.480 --> 00:47:34.330
So you simply, before
you introduce generator,

00:47:34.330 --> 00:47:37.300
you choose at random
between two possibilities,

00:47:37.300 --> 00:47:42.250
down up, up, up, down,
up, or up, up, down,

00:47:42.250 --> 00:47:43.690
very easy to simulate.

00:47:43.690 --> 00:47:47.140
And then here is what you get
with different [INAUDIBLE]..

00:47:47.140 --> 00:47:50.315
On top, with the values you
saw in previous picture,

00:47:50.315 --> 00:47:52.690
you get the sequence which is
more or less what Bachelier

00:47:52.690 --> 00:47:57.990
was saying, more or less what
the conventional so-called

00:47:57.990 --> 00:48:02.650
theory of the market supposes,
coin tossing or a variant

00:48:02.650 --> 00:48:03.780
thereof.

00:48:03.780 --> 00:48:05.800
Then what you do is--

00:48:05.800 --> 00:48:07.380
to come back to
previous thing-- you

00:48:07.380 --> 00:48:10.590
move this top point a
little bit to the left.

00:48:10.590 --> 00:48:15.060
So you have one parameter
here, [INAUDIBLE]

00:48:15.060 --> 00:48:16.160
one point parameter.

00:48:16.160 --> 00:48:20.750
The point parameter is the first
break determines the generator.

00:48:20.750 --> 00:48:24.230
And so as you do it, you
realize that suddenly this thing

00:48:24.230 --> 00:48:26.330
becomes more and more irregular.

00:48:26.330 --> 00:48:27.980
Now, this is all there is to it.

00:48:27.980 --> 00:48:30.530
It is not an
incredible accumulation

00:48:30.530 --> 00:48:33.890
of cycles, epicycles,
not-centered circles,

00:48:33.890 --> 00:48:35.060
et cetera, et cetera.

00:48:35.060 --> 00:48:37.010
It simply is an affinity.

00:48:37.010 --> 00:48:40.490
These curves are increments of
a self-affined process which

00:48:40.490 --> 00:48:42.950
is self-affined by
construction, because you do it

00:48:42.950 --> 00:48:46.160
by recursive process which
has the minimum amount

00:48:46.160 --> 00:48:50.750
of randomness, namely shuffling.

00:48:50.750 --> 00:48:53.150
And at the end,
you get something

00:48:53.150 --> 00:48:55.370
which is no longer
realistic for stock market,

00:48:55.370 --> 00:48:58.160
because it seldom happens that
you have these periods where

00:48:58.160 --> 00:48:59.480
almost nothing happens.

00:48:59.480 --> 00:49:01.820
If you went further,
you get even further.

00:49:01.820 --> 00:49:05.470
So you can go beyond what
you observe in stock market.

00:49:05.470 --> 00:49:06.820
Now, I don't say it's a model.

00:49:06.820 --> 00:49:11.980
I just say that it's a case
in which the first investment

00:49:11.980 --> 00:49:13.630
gives a high yield.

00:49:13.630 --> 00:49:15.730
And I have been
in many sciences.

00:49:15.730 --> 00:49:21.100
And I'm also history nut, so I
read records of many early days

00:49:21.100 --> 00:49:24.190
or many successful
theories in science.

00:49:24.190 --> 00:49:27.790
And there is one theme which
runs through them, which

00:49:27.790 --> 00:49:31.120
is that the first stage
was awfully simple

00:49:31.120 --> 00:49:32.860
and it gave interesting results.

00:49:32.860 --> 00:49:37.300
Then, well, bells and whistles
were added, but only gradually.

00:49:37.300 --> 00:49:39.190
And that those
theories, which began

00:49:39.190 --> 00:49:41.650
by design which was full
of bells and whistles

00:49:41.650 --> 00:49:44.985
to make it look
right, never take off.

00:49:44.985 --> 00:49:47.740
They are just too
overburdened to begin with.

00:49:47.740 --> 00:49:53.310
Now, I don't say this
one is going to take off.

00:49:53.310 --> 00:49:55.530
It's very widely respected.

00:49:55.530 --> 00:49:56.965
It's very widely criticized.

00:49:56.965 --> 00:49:59.320
It's very widely hated.

00:49:59.320 --> 00:50:02.490
It's very widely et
cetera, et cetera.

00:50:02.490 --> 00:50:06.190
But the point is
that it provides

00:50:06.190 --> 00:50:08.920
what I think was
necessary in this context,

00:50:08.920 --> 00:50:13.540
to have an extremely short
no bells, no whistles path

00:50:13.540 --> 00:50:19.540
between folklore and a
quantitative measurable

00:50:19.540 --> 00:50:23.740
and also realistic
representation of data.

00:50:23.740 --> 00:50:27.700
But, now, here is something
which is very disquieting.

00:50:27.700 --> 00:50:31.300
So you see on top of these
things, this is zigzag?

00:50:31.300 --> 00:50:33.220
It's not the same as before.

00:50:33.220 --> 00:50:35.110
But, again, you
just take zigzag,

00:50:35.110 --> 00:50:39.370
and you do it
recursively by changing

00:50:39.370 --> 00:50:41.900
the sequence each time.

00:50:41.900 --> 00:50:46.180
And so these eight
sequences or increments

00:50:46.180 --> 00:50:48.290
are identical from
viewpoint [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:48.290 --> 00:50:50.470
process generates them.

00:50:50.470 --> 00:50:55.870
And here we get into one of
the most constant and the most,

00:50:55.870 --> 00:51:01.330
in fact, perturbing features
of fractal phenomena.

00:51:01.330 --> 00:51:05.790
And perhaps I would first--

00:51:08.511 --> 00:51:09.010
or, no.

00:51:09.010 --> 00:51:10.750
Let me just comment on that.

00:51:10.750 --> 00:51:15.250
If you apply to these eight
processes, the usual techniques

00:51:15.250 --> 00:51:20.510
which are applied to
randomly varying phenomena,

00:51:20.510 --> 00:51:22.390
you find that they're
all different.

00:51:22.390 --> 00:51:23.920
The variance is different.

00:51:23.920 --> 00:51:25.510
The correlation's different.

00:51:25.510 --> 00:51:27.670
Well, in this case,
they're not even white,

00:51:27.670 --> 00:51:30.160
because I didn't make
sure they're white.

00:51:30.160 --> 00:51:32.140
It's very complicated.

00:51:32.140 --> 00:51:34.120
If you apply the right
techniques, which

00:51:34.120 --> 00:51:38.080
are techniques of
multi-fractal analysis

00:51:38.080 --> 00:51:41.200
which is developed for the
purpose of measuring roughness,

00:51:41.200 --> 00:51:43.810
then all these
eight are the same.

00:51:43.810 --> 00:51:46.600
Let me come back now to
this matter of metals

00:51:46.600 --> 00:51:48.110
I mentioned earlier.

00:51:48.110 --> 00:51:51.460
So [? Don Pesoga, ?]
who was a metallurgist,

00:51:51.460 --> 00:51:54.750
was getting from the National
Bureau of Standards--

00:51:54.750 --> 00:51:57.250
or maybe it was already
a different name--

00:51:57.250 --> 00:51:59.250
sort of finger-shaped
piece of metal.

00:51:59.250 --> 00:52:00.910
In fact, they came
in large numbers.

00:52:00.910 --> 00:52:04.150
So the National Bureau
took a piece of metal

00:52:04.150 --> 00:52:06.760
and had an extraordinary careful
treatment, was very expensive

00:52:06.760 --> 00:52:07.690
piece of metal.

00:52:07.690 --> 00:52:11.140
They very carefully treat
it at constant temperature

00:52:11.140 --> 00:52:13.780
or for a long time,
certainly homogeneous.

00:52:13.780 --> 00:52:15.340
Then they're cut
into five pieces

00:52:15.340 --> 00:52:17.980
and sent to different
laboratories who ordered them.

00:52:17.980 --> 00:52:20.380
We [INAUDIBLE] them
bang, bang, bang, bang.

00:52:20.380 --> 00:52:24.280
We measured the roughness
as the book of metallurgy

00:52:24.280 --> 00:52:25.990
said it should be done.

00:52:25.990 --> 00:52:28.390
And that measurement
was, lo and behold,

00:52:28.390 --> 00:52:31.030
exactly the same
as the measurement

00:52:31.030 --> 00:52:33.550
advocate in the
books of finance.

00:52:33.550 --> 00:52:37.360
That is you approximate
this mess by straight line

00:52:37.360 --> 00:52:41.140
or by plane or whatever and
took at the root-mean-square

00:52:41.140 --> 00:52:43.030
deviation from that level.

00:52:43.030 --> 00:52:45.070
And you assume the
root-mean-square deviation

00:52:45.070 --> 00:52:47.860
is a measure of non-flatness.

00:52:47.860 --> 00:52:50.320
Well, it is a measure
of non-flatness.

00:52:50.320 --> 00:52:52.079
But the horrible
fact was that we

00:52:52.079 --> 00:52:54.370
had five samples which the
National Bureau of Standards

00:52:54.370 --> 00:52:55.940
guaranteed to be identical.

00:52:55.940 --> 00:53:01.150
And the measures of roughness
were all over the landscape.

00:53:01.150 --> 00:53:03.382
So they didn't measure anything.

00:53:03.382 --> 00:53:04.840
If you measure the
root-mean-square

00:53:04.840 --> 00:53:08.510
on these things, you get results
completely all over landscape.

00:53:08.510 --> 00:53:10.180
These things are
totally different.

00:53:10.180 --> 00:53:13.990
This suggests an extraordinary
level of variability.

00:53:13.990 --> 00:53:20.350
However, if then measured
for these fractures when you

00:53:20.350 --> 00:53:25.150
measure the fractal dimension--
not quite as simple as what I

00:53:25.150 --> 00:53:28.480
wrote to you, but the next
simplest algorithm which makes

00:53:28.480 --> 00:53:30.050
it measurable--

00:53:30.050 --> 00:53:35.560
we found in all cases, lo and
behold, hold your breath, 4/3.

00:53:35.560 --> 00:53:37.830
Well, not quite 4/3--

00:53:37.830 --> 00:53:39.430
4/3 for lines.

00:53:39.430 --> 00:53:43.870
And it's normal dimensional
line is one, a curve.

00:53:43.870 --> 00:53:45.690
So it's a plus 1/3.

00:53:45.690 --> 00:53:49.500
And for surface,
it was 2 plus 1/3.

00:53:49.500 --> 00:53:51.345
And then people
redid the experiment

00:53:51.345 --> 00:53:53.740
on very much longer samples.

00:53:53.740 --> 00:53:56.920
And they found that,
indeed, the 4/3 [INAUDIBLE]

00:53:56.920 --> 00:53:59.920
were five orders of magnitude.

00:53:59.920 --> 00:54:02.650
That is here is a
case of roughness

00:54:02.650 --> 00:54:06.670
in the most, if you forgive
me, the roughest form,

00:54:06.670 --> 00:54:09.580
the roughness of
broken piece of metal.

00:54:09.580 --> 00:54:12.130
By looking at the
wrong measurements,

00:54:12.130 --> 00:54:15.130
you get values all
over landscape.

00:54:15.130 --> 00:54:16.630
By looking at the
right measurement,

00:54:16.630 --> 00:54:18.440
you find that a
certain value can

00:54:18.440 --> 00:54:22.780
be measured over five or
more orders of magnitude.

00:54:22.780 --> 00:54:26.930
The heat treatment only changes
the crossovers at the end

00:54:26.930 --> 00:54:29.420
and makes this range
bigger or smaller.

00:54:29.420 --> 00:54:33.910
But the precision of that
is absolutely incredible.

00:54:33.910 --> 00:54:36.110
It's also true for glasses.

00:54:36.110 --> 00:54:38.510
It's true for a variety
of extraordinary different

00:54:38.510 --> 00:54:39.710
surfaces.

00:54:39.710 --> 00:54:42.290
Rough surfaces have this
property of plus 1/3.

00:54:42.290 --> 00:54:46.040
Now, I view that has
being, first of all,

00:54:46.040 --> 00:54:50.480
an example of a mixture of
nature reduced to a manageable,

00:54:50.480 --> 00:54:53.810
concise, and clean conjecture.

00:54:53.810 --> 00:54:55.427
It is not a
complicated statement

00:54:55.427 --> 00:54:56.510
about structure of metals.

00:54:56.510 --> 00:54:57.830
It's a simple statement.

00:54:57.830 --> 00:55:00.410
That thing is 4/3.

00:55:00.410 --> 00:55:07.280
And it is, of course, the
greatest clear success,

00:55:07.280 --> 00:55:10.291
greatest token of
success for a model,

00:55:10.291 --> 00:55:12.290
which says that this is
the way to measuring it.

00:55:12.290 --> 00:55:16.250
Now, after having given you
this example, this construction,

00:55:16.250 --> 00:55:19.010
I want to describe
these diagrams.

00:55:19.010 --> 00:55:21.890
In physics, it's very
important that once

00:55:21.890 --> 00:55:26.630
you have a certain model to
change some characteristics,

00:55:26.630 --> 00:55:28.700
temperature and something
else, into a phase

00:55:28.700 --> 00:55:32.210
diagram in which one sees how
the properties of a system

00:55:32.210 --> 00:55:33.170
vary.

00:55:33.170 --> 00:55:37.030
And so these are three ways of
showing it for the symmetric--

00:55:37.030 --> 00:55:40.400
again, coming back the prices
of symmetric generators.

00:55:40.400 --> 00:55:47.820
And, well, let me show you
this and then come back

00:55:47.820 --> 00:55:49.320
to the previous thing.

00:55:49.320 --> 00:55:52.370
This is, on the
left and the right,

00:55:52.370 --> 00:55:56.670
a test which tests whether
price changes indeed [INAUDIBLE]

00:55:56.670 --> 00:55:58.830
follow the multi-fractal model.

00:55:58.830 --> 00:56:02.460
Here, the price in question
is the exchange rate

00:56:02.460 --> 00:56:05.670
between the dollar
and Deutsche Mark

00:56:05.670 --> 00:56:09.460
which had the advantage of being
a rather clean finite sample,

00:56:09.460 --> 00:56:12.390
since on January
1st it will stop,

00:56:12.390 --> 00:56:14.940
since the Deutsche
Mark will vanish.

00:56:14.940 --> 00:56:19.590
But it is known for many
periods with excruciating

00:56:19.590 --> 00:56:21.656
precision every 20 seconds.

00:56:21.656 --> 00:56:23.280
You can have data in
this context which

00:56:23.280 --> 00:56:26.780
are data like one never
thought one could find

00:56:26.780 --> 00:56:29.330
in the context of
economics or finance,

00:56:29.330 --> 00:56:31.820
the data like in physics
in the best cases.

00:56:31.820 --> 00:56:34.290
So one does this diagram.

00:56:34.290 --> 00:56:37.380
And the tau of q is the
conventional name now

00:56:37.380 --> 00:56:40.300
for the slopes of that thing.

00:56:40.300 --> 00:56:42.440
I don't want take time for that.

00:56:42.440 --> 00:56:46.000
On the right, there is this
envelope of these lines.

00:56:46.000 --> 00:56:48.190
And this envelope is
called f of alpha.

00:56:48.190 --> 00:56:52.230
And these things are the
fundamental characteristics

00:56:52.230 --> 00:56:54.130
of the processes.

00:56:54.130 --> 00:56:59.920
So to come back to
these, if one did

00:56:59.920 --> 00:57:01.930
the same analysis
on these eight,

00:57:01.930 --> 00:57:04.480
you find the same results.

00:57:04.480 --> 00:57:06.460
Again, every analysis
[INAUDIBLE] you

00:57:06.460 --> 00:57:08.150
find very different results.

00:57:08.150 --> 00:57:13.570
So here is this matter of a
Manichaean view of the world.

00:57:13.570 --> 00:57:16.470
When I described to you in
the beginning that rough

00:57:16.470 --> 00:57:18.940
versus smooth, of
course I have in mind

00:57:18.940 --> 00:57:23.140
the fact that even
though smoothness

00:57:23.140 --> 00:57:29.310
is so rare in our experience it
was transformed into geometry

00:57:29.310 --> 00:57:30.580
in a marvelous fashion.

00:57:30.580 --> 00:57:33.160
And I was, myself as a
young man, totally immersed

00:57:33.160 --> 00:57:36.490
in classical geometry, the
most wonderful thing one

00:57:36.490 --> 00:57:37.440
could imagine.

00:57:37.440 --> 00:57:42.670
And by this kind of
extraordinary miracle,

00:57:42.670 --> 00:57:45.760
it gave rise to a description
of nature beginning

00:57:45.760 --> 00:57:50.770
by apsis being good for Kepler
for describing planet's motion,

00:57:50.770 --> 00:57:52.330
et cetera, et cetera.

00:57:52.330 --> 00:57:55.240
The whole of science is
dominated by a mathematics

00:57:55.240 --> 00:57:57.630
which is that of the smooth.

00:57:57.630 --> 00:58:00.040
And that mathematics
arose early on.

00:58:00.040 --> 00:58:03.880
Derivatives, of course, are
part of it very strongly.

00:58:03.880 --> 00:58:07.750
And most differential
manifolds and so on, you

00:58:07.750 --> 00:58:09.920
find these words
differentiable all the time.

00:58:09.920 --> 00:58:13.600
These are the primary objects of
mathematics and, in particular,

00:58:13.600 --> 00:58:20.140
of science in
gravitation and light.

00:58:20.140 --> 00:58:23.260
But, now, we have
the situation that

00:58:23.260 --> 00:58:28.090
in some parts of nature things
don't fit in this mold at all.

00:58:28.090 --> 00:58:31.530
So by this belated
process, which

00:58:31.530 --> 00:58:35.160
started with very, very
abstract mathematics,

00:58:35.160 --> 00:58:37.770
was trying to get
separate from physics.

00:58:37.770 --> 00:58:39.960
Again, remember these
words of Cantor,

00:58:39.960 --> 00:58:42.090
which I find totally
extraordinary,

00:58:42.090 --> 00:58:45.390
that mathematicians can
free themselves from nature.

00:58:45.390 --> 00:58:47.260
By trying to free
themselves from nature,

00:58:47.260 --> 00:58:51.630
they introduce these
concepts and related ideas

00:58:51.630 --> 00:58:55.080
which were called mathematical
monsters and the like.

00:58:55.080 --> 00:59:00.090
Poincaré was making fun
of Cantor and [INAUDIBLE]

00:59:00.090 --> 00:59:03.090
by saying that in the past
mathematicians were inventing

00:59:03.090 --> 00:59:05.820
new shapes to help
us understand nature,

00:59:05.820 --> 00:59:08.550
but today they invent new
shapes just to annoy me.

00:59:08.550 --> 00:59:10.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:10.410 --> 00:59:12.490
Well, it was the case.

00:59:12.490 --> 00:59:18.350
And so the central point
of fractal geometry is that

00:59:18.350 --> 00:59:22.400
actually Cantor was
just plain dead wrong,

00:59:22.400 --> 00:59:24.200
that these shapes were not--

00:59:24.200 --> 00:59:25.910
the way they're
invented is irrelevant.

00:59:25.910 --> 00:59:31.160
Whether he knew of decorative
schemes or not is irrelevant.

00:59:31.160 --> 00:59:34.330
These mathematics was
actually necessary to take

00:59:34.330 --> 00:59:37.490
the first step for
study of roughness.

00:59:37.490 --> 00:59:43.310
And so that brings, again,
into very strong relief

00:59:43.310 --> 00:59:47.990
this question of averaging,
which destroys randomness.

00:59:47.990 --> 00:59:50.839
The classical pattern was
that of thermodynamics.

00:59:50.839 --> 00:59:52.880
I don't know that anybody
teaches that any longer

00:59:52.880 --> 00:59:54.838
or classical thermodynamics
in which randomness

00:59:54.838 --> 00:59:56.000
[? didn't ?] exist.

00:59:56.000 --> 00:59:58.180
And then there was
statistical thermodynamics

00:59:58.180 --> 01:00:00.110
in which energy
was not a constant,

01:00:00.110 --> 01:00:02.300
but was fluctuating
a little bit--

01:00:02.300 --> 01:00:03.530
but so little.

01:00:03.530 --> 01:00:06.500
Only refined
electronic measurements

01:00:06.500 --> 01:00:08.120
could pick the thermal noise.

01:00:08.120 --> 01:00:12.260
And that was discovered
only rather recently,

01:00:12.260 --> 01:00:15.210
namely about the
time I was born.

01:00:15.210 --> 01:00:19.070
For everybody, that is the
definition of recently.

01:00:19.070 --> 01:00:23.000
But the phenomena we deal
here are not like that.

01:00:23.000 --> 01:00:24.710
Things don't average out.

01:00:24.710 --> 01:00:28.880
And so what I have developed
increasingly over the years

01:00:28.880 --> 01:00:33.500
gradually, but focus
now very strongly, is--

01:00:33.500 --> 01:00:35.960
which I would like to
give you more or less

01:00:35.960 --> 01:00:37.100
to finish this talk--

01:00:37.100 --> 01:00:39.030
of states of randomness.

01:00:39.030 --> 01:00:42.710
Now, in mechanics, we all agree
that the laws of mechanics

01:00:42.710 --> 01:00:44.000
are unique.

01:00:44.000 --> 01:00:47.210
Well, forget about
details for the phenomena

01:00:47.210 --> 01:00:48.110
we are looking at.

01:00:48.110 --> 01:00:51.380
It's all classical
mechanics, Newton and so on.

01:00:51.380 --> 01:00:52.100
Laws are unique.

01:00:52.100 --> 01:00:55.540
However, if you look at
an assembly of molecules,

01:00:55.540 --> 01:00:58.410
it matters very much whether
the temperature and pressure

01:00:58.410 --> 01:01:02.840
as such, that the whole thing is
a liquid, a gas, or a crystal,

01:01:02.840 --> 01:01:05.130
or a solid as I'm
used to saying.

01:01:05.130 --> 01:01:06.290
It matters very greatly.

01:01:06.290 --> 01:01:08.980
So one does not begin
by studying everything

01:01:08.980 --> 01:01:09.830
at the initial.

01:01:09.830 --> 01:01:13.070
When first it's a gas, I
know which tools to use.

01:01:13.070 --> 01:01:13.690
It's a liquid.

01:01:13.690 --> 01:01:15.890
I know which tools use
even though liquids

01:01:15.890 --> 01:01:18.060
are pretty horrible stuff.

01:01:18.060 --> 01:01:20.920
And solid, I know
which tools to use.

01:01:20.920 --> 01:01:25.070
Now, in randomness, this
was not at all emphasized

01:01:25.070 --> 01:01:30.542
that there is a beautiful
axiomatic good for everybody.

01:01:30.542 --> 01:01:32.750
But then once you go to
practice, especially practice

01:01:32.750 --> 01:01:36.560
in physics and
engineering and finance,

01:01:36.560 --> 01:01:41.300
one always look to phenomenon
which averages everything

01:01:41.300 --> 01:01:44.075
so they're averaged out
to limits very quickly.

01:01:44.075 --> 01:01:47.060
There was no randomness
after a certain time.

01:01:47.060 --> 01:01:53.180
But the phenomena which I
study, whether it is in nature--

01:01:53.180 --> 01:01:57.560
and that means
turbulence or whatever--

01:01:57.560 --> 01:02:00.860
or in culture that is
stock market or everywhere,

01:02:00.860 --> 01:02:06.440
these phenomena do not give any
evidence of being averaging.

01:02:06.440 --> 01:02:10.390
I call them widely random.

01:02:10.390 --> 01:02:20.560
And the very simple
formulas in that context

01:02:20.560 --> 01:02:28.520
give very, very
complicated structures.

01:02:28.520 --> 01:02:30.560
I would like to end
up by one thing.

01:02:30.560 --> 01:02:34.700
I said I was ending, but let
me just add another point.

01:02:34.700 --> 01:02:37.540
At one point, I was very much
attacked by my friends, mostly

01:02:37.540 --> 01:02:38.150
my friends.

01:02:38.150 --> 01:02:40.910
My enemies did not even bother.

01:02:40.910 --> 01:02:44.670
Because I was describing an
alternative view of nature,

01:02:44.670 --> 01:02:47.340
which was so different from
the view based on smoothness.

01:02:47.340 --> 01:02:49.850
Smoothness include,
of course, everything

01:02:49.850 --> 01:02:51.920
which is ruled by
partial differential

01:02:51.920 --> 01:02:57.440
equations, Laplace, Fourier,
Poisson, Levy, Stokes.

01:02:57.440 --> 01:02:59.000
And this thing had
no differential,

01:02:59.000 --> 01:03:02.130
no derivatives, had strange
behavior, and so on.

01:03:02.130 --> 01:03:05.000
So do these things belong
to different universes?

01:03:05.000 --> 01:03:08.510
Well, I don't think you do.

01:03:08.510 --> 01:03:09.920
I think it's just very unified.

01:03:09.920 --> 01:03:12.620
I think that partial
differential equations,

01:03:12.620 --> 01:03:17.630
if pushed beyond the stringent
conditions under which

01:03:17.630 --> 01:03:20.330
solutions are unique and
smooth and differential

01:03:20.330 --> 01:03:24.890
and everything, they give rise
to very complicated behaviors.

01:03:24.890 --> 01:03:26.690
And one example of
it is that if you

01:03:26.690 --> 01:03:30.650
take a large assembly
of particles interacting

01:03:30.650 --> 01:03:35.920
by inverse square root
attraction a la Newton,

01:03:35.920 --> 01:03:41.300
if you start with a Poisson
distribution or just a lattice

01:03:41.300 --> 01:03:44.570
and put some pinch of
energy to start with,

01:03:44.570 --> 01:03:51.100
after a very short time, you
see that these particles become

01:03:51.100 --> 01:03:54.590
extraordinary complicated
and reassemble themselves

01:03:54.590 --> 01:03:56.477
into a fractal universe.

01:03:56.477 --> 01:03:58.310
I didn't mention fractal
universe until now,

01:03:58.310 --> 01:04:00.170
but I will like to say
two words about it.

01:04:00.170 --> 01:04:03.670
The solution of galaxies in
this universe is fractal.

01:04:03.670 --> 01:04:05.240
There is no density.

01:04:05.240 --> 01:04:09.350
All kinds of things occur
which are quite new.

01:04:09.350 --> 01:04:13.280
Well, that would suggest that
the clustering of galaxies

01:04:13.280 --> 01:04:19.130
could very well not be due to
specific complicated forces,

01:04:19.130 --> 01:04:24.140
which we don't know, but the
simple behavior of particles

01:04:24.140 --> 01:04:27.780
under 1 over r
square attraction.

01:04:27.780 --> 01:04:33.750
And so that raise the question,
is clustering up there

01:04:33.750 --> 01:04:34.260
in the sky?

01:04:34.260 --> 01:04:36.180
Or is it up there in the head?

01:04:36.180 --> 01:04:39.630
Well, it may well
be in your head.

01:04:39.630 --> 01:04:41.190
I don't know, but
it's a question

01:04:41.190 --> 01:04:42.970
which has to be discussed.

01:04:42.970 --> 01:04:45.960
In fact, one finds that
many of these questions

01:04:45.960 --> 01:04:48.410
become very, very much revised.

01:04:48.410 --> 01:04:51.210
Now, if one begins to
include the fractal

01:04:51.210 --> 01:04:52.470
of the universe-- oh, yes.

01:04:52.470 --> 01:04:53.620
It's a fact.

01:04:53.620 --> 01:04:58.040
Early on, which is sort of 20
years ago when I wrote my book,

01:04:58.040 --> 01:05:00.160
the universe was
expected to be fractal

01:05:00.160 --> 01:05:03.560
up to 5 megaparsecs, 50
million light years depth.

01:05:03.560 --> 01:05:06.030
It's nothing by
universal standards.

01:05:06.030 --> 01:05:09.270
But then it crept
to 20 to 50 to 100.

01:05:09.270 --> 01:05:09.900
I don't know.

01:05:09.900 --> 01:05:12.080
It's between 200 and
300 for most people now.

01:05:12.080 --> 01:05:15.570
And many people believe it's
1,000, which is pretty far.

01:05:15.570 --> 01:05:18.660
So if it is true that
the universe is fractal

01:05:18.660 --> 01:05:21.870
up to that level,
then fractality

01:05:21.870 --> 01:05:25.000
will insert itself
into cosmology

01:05:25.000 --> 01:05:27.072
in a very strong fashion.

01:05:27.072 --> 01:05:28.530
Therefore, not only
fractality will

01:05:28.530 --> 01:05:30.510
be a result of very
simple laws of physics,

01:05:30.510 --> 01:05:33.150
like 1 over r squared
possibly, but will

01:05:33.150 --> 01:05:36.360
affect our understanding
about [INAUDIBLE]..

01:05:36.360 --> 01:05:39.300
Well, I must stop here, because
things got more complicated,

01:05:39.300 --> 01:05:40.590
become very complicated.

01:05:40.590 --> 01:05:41.990
And this is not
for this lecture.

01:05:41.990 --> 01:05:44.490
I've been writing on this topic
for a very long time, as you

01:05:44.490 --> 01:05:46.325
see.

01:05:46.325 --> 01:05:48.450
And, also, for those who
are interested in finance,

01:05:48.450 --> 01:05:49.980
the Journal of
Quantitative Finance

01:05:49.980 --> 01:05:55.030
has several papers in
the current year on that.

01:05:55.030 --> 01:05:56.850
I publish right and left.

01:05:56.850 --> 01:05:59.694
That's my book on finance,
my book on [INAUDIBLE] noise.

01:05:59.694 --> 01:06:01.860
I would like to end by this
one, which is coming out

01:06:01.860 --> 01:06:05.420
in a few weeks or a few
days perhaps by Frame

01:06:05.420 --> 01:06:09.540
and myself on
mathematics education.

01:06:09.540 --> 01:06:16.926
We find to our utter amazement
that humans like fractals.

01:06:16.926 --> 01:06:19.530
[LAUGHTER]

01:06:19.530 --> 01:06:22.690
And I won't mention
anything about art,

01:06:22.690 --> 01:06:26.490
but this was the result
of a very, very, very

01:06:26.490 --> 01:06:28.290
poor programmers work.

01:06:28.290 --> 01:06:32.310
He had some bug, a semicolon
or something dreadful.

01:06:32.310 --> 01:06:33.360
It gives the result.

01:06:33.360 --> 01:06:36.820
He start apologizing,
fearing to be fired.

01:06:36.820 --> 01:06:39.060
I start thanking from
bottom of my heart.

01:06:39.060 --> 01:06:44.820
Before correcting the bug,
we had his picture taken.

01:06:44.820 --> 01:06:47.700
It's something around the
Mandelbrot set mistake

01:06:47.700 --> 01:06:48.650
was made.

01:06:48.650 --> 01:06:50.910
But it's a whole
topic unto itself

01:06:50.910 --> 01:06:58.110
that humans seem to be
happy with roughness.

01:07:00.350 --> 01:07:02.100
You must have the same
experience as I do.

01:07:02.100 --> 01:07:05.910
Because you say a
mathematician, oh, people say,

01:07:05.910 --> 01:07:10.350
I find mathematics
utterly dry and dull.

01:07:10.350 --> 01:07:13.180
Well, I don't, but they do.

01:07:13.180 --> 01:07:16.830
But this is one
part of mathematics.

01:07:16.830 --> 01:07:20.530
And that's mathematics of
the flat and the smooth.

01:07:20.530 --> 01:07:26.570
The mathematics of the rough,
as developed by many people 100

01:07:26.570 --> 01:07:30.830
years ago and now it's galloping
development on all sides,

01:07:30.830 --> 01:07:32.550
never gets this reaction.

01:07:32.550 --> 01:07:36.110
In fact, they say, if only
I had that in high school,

01:07:36.110 --> 01:07:37.550
I would have loved mathematics.

01:07:37.550 --> 01:07:40.670
Well, you don't need to be
told that, that mathematics is

01:07:40.670 --> 01:07:41.300
lovable.

01:07:41.300 --> 01:07:45.810
But it's only at
my old age that I

01:07:45.810 --> 01:07:49.440
dare present my life
and the story of science

01:07:49.440 --> 01:07:54.290
as being a conflict between two
forces, one force good and bad,

01:07:54.290 --> 01:07:55.140
the evil.

01:07:55.140 --> 01:07:58.500
You choose which one's
which, smooth and rough.

01:07:58.500 --> 01:08:02.010
But I think it has illuminated
my view of how things occurred.

01:08:02.010 --> 01:08:05.610
And the reason why roughness
came so late, why so difficult,

01:08:05.610 --> 01:08:08.186
is because it simply
is more difficult.

01:08:08.186 --> 01:08:09.810
It requires difficult
mathematics which

01:08:09.810 --> 01:08:11.700
was developed only
very recently, which

01:08:11.700 --> 01:08:15.640
is developed now for the
purpose of this investigation.

01:08:15.640 --> 01:08:18.310
It requires just a
different viewpoint.

01:08:18.310 --> 01:08:24.149
But it is, I think,
definitely a frontier

01:08:24.149 --> 01:08:27.454
of scientific investigation.

01:08:27.454 --> 01:08:28.438
Thank you.

01:08:28.438 --> 01:08:30.406
[APPLAUSE]

01:08:44.988 --> 01:08:46.779
MODERATOR: Thank you,
Professor Mandelbrot.

01:08:46.779 --> 01:08:49.609
We have time for
a few questions.

01:08:49.609 --> 01:08:51.970
We have two microphones
up here, but you've

01:08:51.970 --> 01:08:54.939
managed to fill in the
density of the auditorium

01:08:54.939 --> 01:08:57.310
sufficiently high that we
may have some trouble getting

01:08:57.310 --> 01:08:58.370
to them.

01:08:58.370 --> 01:09:02.529
So if you want to ask without
the benefit of amplification,

01:09:02.529 --> 01:09:05.319
please speak out
as well as you can.

01:09:05.319 --> 01:09:06.722
Do we have any questions?

01:09:09.769 --> 01:09:10.310
One over here

01:09:10.310 --> 01:09:12.393
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
Mandelbrot, as a high school

01:09:12.393 --> 01:09:21.270
teacher who is trying to
get all students to be

01:09:21.270 --> 01:09:25.880
good mathematicians, to
apply themselves and so on,

01:09:25.880 --> 01:09:31.381
how much of practical
[INAUDIBLE] can I do?

01:09:31.381 --> 01:09:32.380
MANDELBROT: [INAUDIBLE].

01:09:32.380 --> 01:09:35.450
I think much more than
you think and much more

01:09:35.450 --> 01:09:38.990
than I thought not so long ago.

01:09:38.990 --> 01:09:41.669
We have now for
several years at Yale

01:09:41.669 --> 01:09:44.352
a workshop during the summer
for high school teachers.

01:09:44.352 --> 01:09:45.810
So we get the cream
of Connecticut.

01:09:45.810 --> 01:09:47.819
So they are the best.

01:09:47.819 --> 01:09:50.560
But they tell us stories
which are quite incredible.

01:09:50.560 --> 01:09:55.140
And they ask us about
how early can I bring it.

01:09:55.140 --> 01:09:56.820
Is junior high right time?

01:09:56.820 --> 01:09:58.992
Some people say
elementary school.

01:09:58.992 --> 01:10:00.450
One of our friends--
we have a kind

01:10:00.450 --> 01:10:02.820
of network of friends
around who have

01:10:02.820 --> 01:10:04.290
been using these techniques.

01:10:04.290 --> 01:10:09.000
Some of our friends have tried
with the kindergarten children.

01:10:09.000 --> 01:10:10.360
They put them blocks.

01:10:10.360 --> 01:10:15.420
The blocks had O or I or X.
And children played with that.

01:10:15.420 --> 01:10:17.580
Then you put other
fractal things.

01:10:17.580 --> 01:10:21.600
And the challenge is to
rebuild the fractal by putting

01:10:21.600 --> 01:10:23.010
these things in order.

01:10:23.010 --> 01:10:25.500
The kids become so
absorbed, apparently,

01:10:25.500 --> 01:10:26.850
that they are lost in.

01:10:26.850 --> 01:10:28.660
Because that's a real task.

01:10:28.660 --> 01:10:32.260
It is not a task which you,
the teacher, has imposed.

01:10:32.260 --> 01:10:35.820
It's a task that nature has
imposed on humanity forever

01:10:35.820 --> 01:10:37.140
and forever.

01:10:37.140 --> 01:10:39.450
And so in junior
high, it's very easy.

01:10:39.450 --> 01:10:41.070
Everybody who's
anybody knows how

01:10:41.070 --> 01:10:44.010
to promote at 11, which puts
me to shame because I never

01:10:44.010 --> 01:10:47.570
did learn to program.

01:10:47.570 --> 01:10:49.920
So it should be early.

01:10:49.920 --> 01:10:52.355
In a certain sense, the
question of whether this

01:10:52.355 --> 01:10:53.730
is the right
mathematics to begin

01:10:53.730 --> 01:10:57.630
with is the wrong question,
because the mathematics

01:10:57.630 --> 01:11:00.420
with smooth had
been so elaborated,

01:11:00.420 --> 01:11:03.360
had become very, very
far from experience.

01:11:03.360 --> 01:11:08.570
And the questions it asks
are so refined that it is not

01:11:08.570 --> 01:11:10.555
questions which come naturally.

01:11:10.555 --> 01:11:14.730
They're not questions
about anything [INAUDIBLE]

01:11:14.730 --> 01:11:15.630
had thought.

01:11:15.630 --> 01:11:18.660
The Pascal theorem
about the circles--

01:11:18.660 --> 01:11:20.850
I was a geometry nut.

01:11:20.850 --> 01:11:21.510
I loved that.

01:11:21.510 --> 01:11:23.179
But I was the only
one who loved it.

01:11:23.179 --> 01:11:24.970
For all the others, it
was artificial thing

01:11:24.970 --> 01:11:27.750
that somebody names Pascal
just meant to bother them.

01:11:27.750 --> 01:11:31.860
But the question about
mastering roughness

01:11:31.860 --> 01:11:35.280
which I have to-- again, the
rule and not the exception.

01:11:35.280 --> 01:11:37.560
Smoothness is engineering.

01:11:37.560 --> 01:11:39.820
This table is smooth,
well, not quite so smooth,

01:11:39.820 --> 01:11:42.010
because it's already
several years there.

01:11:42.010 --> 01:11:43.960
So it has some roughness,
I'm sure fractal.

01:11:43.960 --> 01:11:46.470
But with approximation,
it's smooth.

01:11:46.470 --> 01:11:48.060
It's engineering,
which is cold, which

01:11:48.060 --> 01:11:50.320
is dry, et cetera, et cetera.

01:11:50.320 --> 01:11:53.820
So if mathematics can
go back to its origins--

01:11:53.820 --> 01:11:57.270
and it is not even something
which is artificial.

01:11:57.270 --> 01:11:59.801
For example, I mean,
give an example why.

01:11:59.801 --> 01:12:01.800
I'm discussing that with
a friend the other day.

01:12:01.800 --> 01:12:03.900
I introduced a notion
which was taken

01:12:03.900 --> 01:12:08.340
as being the end of madness,
that of negative dimension.

01:12:08.340 --> 01:12:10.800
Now, you measure things,
so you measure roughness.

01:12:10.800 --> 01:12:14.970
And, now, you can also
measure vacuity, emptiness.

01:12:14.970 --> 01:12:16.590
So listen to that.

01:12:16.590 --> 01:12:19.880
If you take two lines
in [INAUDIBLE] space,

01:12:19.880 --> 01:12:21.550
their intersection--

01:12:21.550 --> 01:12:23.550
well, they [INAUDIBLE]
intersect, of course.

01:12:23.550 --> 01:12:26.730
But intersection
of lines is less

01:12:26.730 --> 01:12:29.700
empty than intersection of line
and a point or of two points.

01:12:29.700 --> 01:12:31.510
That looks like science fiction.

01:12:31.510 --> 01:12:34.860
Actually, it's a question which
arose when friends of mine

01:12:34.860 --> 01:12:38.520
were looking at intermittency
of turbulence in the laboratory,

01:12:38.520 --> 01:12:39.837
because--

01:12:39.837 --> 01:12:40.670
I could explain why.

01:12:40.670 --> 01:12:41.760
It's a long story.

01:12:41.760 --> 01:12:45.120
But then to explain
it, we must understand

01:12:45.120 --> 01:12:47.100
that lines are not lines.

01:12:47.100 --> 01:12:48.990
Lines the little tubes.

01:12:48.990 --> 01:12:52.880
And lines being little tubes is
not the only invention of me.

01:12:52.880 --> 01:12:54.960
It's the invention
of Minkowski, who

01:12:54.960 --> 01:12:57.750
was a very great man in
1900 and who point out

01:12:57.750 --> 01:13:00.540
that one gets away from all
kinds of horrible paradoxes

01:13:00.540 --> 01:13:04.840
in mathematics by never
thinking of lines are lines

01:13:04.840 --> 01:13:07.350
or of surface as surface, but
thinking of lines as being

01:13:07.350 --> 01:13:09.262
tubes, surfaces as
being [INAUDIBLE] et

01:13:09.262 --> 01:13:10.290
cetera, et cetera.

01:13:10.290 --> 01:13:12.960
So one goes back
to these things.

01:13:12.960 --> 01:13:18.660
Not only one goes back to Mother
Earth to get strength again,

01:13:18.660 --> 01:13:21.210
but one does in a fashion
which are fruitful.

01:13:21.210 --> 01:13:24.270
Because without this
element of going back

01:13:24.270 --> 01:13:27.590
into epsilon neighborhoods,
to call them by a fancy term,

01:13:27.590 --> 01:13:29.910
one could not make sense
of the negative dimension.

01:13:29.910 --> 01:13:32.250
And negative dimension is
something you can measure

01:13:32.250 --> 01:13:36.886
and which is a useful thing
in the study of turbulence.

01:13:36.886 --> 01:13:38.510
MODERATOR: Do we have
another question?

01:13:41.432 --> 01:13:45.220
AUDIENCE: Are there any human
behavior in fractal terms?

01:13:45.220 --> 01:13:51.760
MANDELBROT: Well, certainly,
stock market is human behavior.

01:13:51.760 --> 01:13:54.820
As a matter of fact, I have
come to use the word culture

01:13:54.820 --> 01:13:59.980
in a sense, which is perhaps
not smooth to some ears

01:13:59.980 --> 01:14:01.810
speaking fractal
geometry of nature

01:14:01.810 --> 01:14:03.880
and then giving an
example like stock market.

01:14:03.880 --> 01:14:07.930
But take example of internet.

01:14:07.930 --> 01:14:08.830
It's human behavior.

01:14:08.830 --> 01:14:11.230
It's not exactly what you
think of human behavior.

01:14:11.230 --> 01:14:13.690
But a large number of
very brilliant people

01:14:13.690 --> 01:14:16.284
working at cross purposes
put this thing together.

01:14:16.284 --> 01:14:17.950
And it works marvelous
most of the time.

01:14:17.950 --> 01:14:19.870
And every so often,
it's terrible

01:14:19.870 --> 01:14:22.440
and your messages
don't go through.

01:14:22.440 --> 01:14:25.060
So people first try to
apply to the internet

01:14:25.060 --> 01:14:29.830
the techniques which had worked
for telephones with Poisson

01:14:29.830 --> 01:14:30.910
behavior and so on.

01:14:30.910 --> 01:14:33.340
It was absolutely off the mark.

01:14:33.340 --> 01:14:34.559
It is a multi-fractal.

01:14:34.559 --> 01:14:35.850
And it has to be multi-fractal.

01:14:35.850 --> 01:14:37.900
Now, after the fact,
it's understood.

01:14:37.900 --> 01:14:39.100
So here is human behavior.

01:14:39.100 --> 01:14:43.720
I mean, because design of
this very informal, and noisy,

01:14:43.720 --> 01:14:46.210
and messy design of
this huge system, which

01:14:46.210 --> 01:14:48.360
for reasons which
are, after the fact,

01:14:48.360 --> 01:14:51.190
sort of vaguely understood,
is multi-fractal.

01:14:51.190 --> 01:14:53.500
But you don't have to
understand it to live with it,

01:14:53.500 --> 01:14:55.100
because you have
to live with it.

01:14:55.100 --> 01:14:57.310
And so if you're
making equipment,

01:14:57.310 --> 01:15:01.730
you better test whether
its properties behaves well

01:15:01.730 --> 01:15:03.520
in the face of it.

01:15:03.520 --> 01:15:07.200
But I was trying to start
with human behavior, which

01:15:07.200 --> 01:15:09.010
is not usually called as such.

01:15:09.010 --> 01:15:11.720
But art is certainly
human behavior.

01:15:11.720 --> 01:15:15.200
And so does art have
fractal aspects?

01:15:15.200 --> 01:15:16.320
Certainly.

01:15:16.320 --> 01:15:19.730
And let me give you an
example of that, which I still

01:15:19.730 --> 01:15:22.910
find mind-boggling.

01:15:22.910 --> 01:15:26.450
My friend Richard Voss
found experimentally

01:15:26.450 --> 01:15:29.390
that music is 1 over F
noise like these things

01:15:29.390 --> 01:15:30.810
I was showing you here.

01:15:30.810 --> 01:15:32.010
He's always a physicist.

01:15:32.010 --> 01:15:35.780
And he is not a loud person,
so it didn't become very widely

01:15:35.780 --> 01:15:36.620
known.

01:15:36.620 --> 01:15:38.360
But then I was
approached independently

01:15:38.360 --> 01:15:42.350
by two composers, one in
New York and one in Europe.

01:15:42.350 --> 01:15:45.380
And you must know the names,
Charles Warren and Gyorgy

01:15:45.380 --> 01:15:49.430
Ligeti, who told me that
looking at my pictures

01:15:49.430 --> 01:15:53.560
made them understand
nature of their craft.

01:15:53.560 --> 01:15:55.510
And I asked for
each day, how come?

01:15:55.510 --> 01:15:57.570
And they say, well, I--

01:15:57.570 --> 01:16:00.330
I speak in the voice of
either, because exactly

01:16:00.330 --> 01:16:01.480
the same explanation.

01:16:01.480 --> 01:16:04.146
I have been trained in a
very classical conservatory

01:16:04.146 --> 01:16:06.520
tradition, so I knew very well
about all the instruments,

01:16:06.520 --> 01:16:09.250
their values, about everything.

01:16:09.250 --> 01:16:11.180
But I was not told
one basic thing,

01:16:11.180 --> 01:16:13.540
which is what distinguishes
a piece of music

01:16:13.540 --> 01:16:16.120
from a collection of noises.

01:16:16.120 --> 01:16:22.630
And I learned, each of them
said, by trial and error.

01:16:22.630 --> 01:16:24.220
I brought a composition
to my teacher.

01:16:24.220 --> 01:16:26.860
He said, oh, too busy.

01:16:26.860 --> 01:16:29.290
And I brought
another composition.

01:16:29.290 --> 01:16:30.880
It's not decorated enough.

01:16:30.880 --> 01:16:34.450
And another proposition, it
just goes up and down too much,

01:16:34.450 --> 01:16:35.890
doesn't go up and not too much.

01:16:35.890 --> 01:16:38.250
After a while, I finally
understood what to do.

01:16:38.250 --> 01:16:39.750
And I've been doing
that very well.

01:16:39.750 --> 01:16:43.550
And I am sure that you
know Ligeti, and Warren.

01:16:43.550 --> 01:16:45.527
They are very famous people.

01:16:45.527 --> 01:16:47.860
And then I look at the picture,
I say, but it's obvious.

01:16:47.860 --> 01:16:49.780
It's obvious, except
nobody told me that.

01:16:49.780 --> 01:16:51.250
It's multi-fractal.

01:16:51.250 --> 01:16:56.530
That is a sonata, 21 minutes,
three movements, allegro,

01:16:56.530 --> 01:17:00.190
lento, presto, different.

01:17:00.190 --> 01:17:06.880
Each movement-- loud,
soft, very loud.

01:17:06.880 --> 01:17:09.640
So the thing must have
structure at all scales.

01:17:09.640 --> 01:17:11.740
Something will be
changing at every scale.

01:17:11.740 --> 01:17:12.700
And that's it.

01:17:12.700 --> 01:17:15.160
If you get that, you
get at least bad music.

01:17:15.160 --> 01:17:17.060
[LAUGHTER]

01:17:17.060 --> 01:17:19.540
And bad music is much
better than noise.

01:17:19.540 --> 01:17:21.610
And everybody think by music.

01:17:21.610 --> 01:17:24.010
And Voss was telling
a story about he

01:17:24.010 --> 01:17:26.965
did some pentatonic
music and the Chinese say

01:17:26.965 --> 01:17:29.800
it's bad Korean music,
Korean bad Japanese music,

01:17:29.800 --> 01:17:32.550
Japanese bad Thai
music, whatever,

01:17:32.550 --> 01:17:37.690
but it was music of
some barbarian race.

01:17:37.690 --> 01:17:40.480
So that essence, it's not
at all fundamental, again,

01:17:40.480 --> 01:17:42.230
good and bad music
[INAUDIBLE] together.

01:17:42.230 --> 01:17:44.740
Now, so I can go on and on.

01:17:44.740 --> 01:17:51.500
Painting-- so in the
classical landscape, which

01:17:51.500 --> 01:17:54.990
is very artificial form of art
both in oriental and European

01:17:54.990 --> 01:17:57.990
tradition, so there was big
tree which framed it and then

01:17:57.990 --> 01:17:59.622
this little man and so on.

01:17:59.622 --> 01:18:01.330
There are all these
rules of composition.

01:18:01.330 --> 01:18:02.550
And you look at it--

01:18:02.550 --> 01:18:03.540
one big thing.

01:18:03.540 --> 01:18:07.570
And most of these books of
art did speak of design.

01:18:07.570 --> 01:18:09.240
And I tell you what
I did discover.

01:18:09.240 --> 01:18:11.610
Oh, I do my own
covers for my books.

01:18:11.610 --> 01:18:16.050
It's kind of-- well,
I like to do it.

01:18:16.050 --> 01:18:20.730
So this book for teaching
has every single cliche

01:18:20.730 --> 01:18:25.140
of composition, because I
felt it would be effective.

01:18:25.140 --> 01:18:26.850
But on the other
hand, I was looking

01:18:26.850 --> 01:18:29.940
at the film of Kandinsky
painting on his paintings.

01:18:29.940 --> 01:18:35.220
It looked exactly like a program
to do fractals would look.

01:18:35.220 --> 01:18:37.110
He would look at
this piece of a thing

01:18:37.110 --> 01:18:42.090
and then boom, a big
line, solid line.

01:18:42.090 --> 01:18:44.520
You see that in all
kinds of composition.

01:18:44.520 --> 01:18:46.830
Then he would set small lines.

01:18:46.830 --> 01:18:50.280
When the film ends,
has a small brush

01:18:50.280 --> 01:18:52.950
and he's adding little
things all over the place.

01:18:52.950 --> 01:18:55.320
So he puts big and small
and medium and so on

01:18:55.320 --> 01:18:57.180
elements of scale
which is exactly

01:18:57.180 --> 01:19:01.260
the kind of basic
bottom multi-fractality.

01:19:01.260 --> 01:19:03.040
I could go on
forever and forever.

01:19:05.580 --> 01:19:07.610
This, I'm speaking
of current behavior.

01:19:07.610 --> 01:19:11.490
But if you look at
this African village--

01:19:11.490 --> 01:19:15.630
so this behavior is not in
the sense of a physiologist,

01:19:15.630 --> 01:19:17.650
but a sense of the artist.

01:19:17.650 --> 01:19:19.660
Now, physiologist,
that's another matter.

01:19:19.660 --> 01:19:22.110
Now, many of my friends
tell me that they

01:19:22.110 --> 01:19:23.550
have a [INAUDIBLE]
recordings look

01:19:23.550 --> 01:19:24.960
like these things I showed.

01:19:24.960 --> 01:19:27.400
I'd be very much
interested in seeing them.

01:19:27.400 --> 01:19:31.980
But I know very well that if
you describe some surfaces

01:19:31.980 --> 01:19:37.440
in the body that is
certainly human behavior.

01:19:37.440 --> 01:19:40.380
Some surfaces are meant
to be as small as possible

01:19:40.380 --> 01:19:43.410
given their volume and
others as big as possible.

01:19:43.410 --> 01:19:45.570
The skin should be
smooth and taught,

01:19:45.570 --> 01:19:48.210
like a young child's skin.

01:19:48.210 --> 01:19:53.080
And the lung inside must
be as confused as possible.

01:19:53.080 --> 01:19:59.340
So the two criteria of design
lead to either smooth shapes

01:19:59.340 --> 01:20:02.640
or to extremely convoluted
shapes, which happen

01:20:02.640 --> 01:20:04.440
to have fractal features.

01:20:04.440 --> 01:20:09.660
The human lung, for example, is
branching fractal on 23 levels.

01:20:09.660 --> 01:20:12.960
23 levels is a very
healthy bifurcation.

01:20:12.960 --> 01:20:14.280
It's a very healthy number.

01:20:14.280 --> 01:20:17.681
It's a [? million. ?]

01:20:17.681 --> 01:20:20.180
MODERATOR: I think we're going
to need to wrap up right now,

01:20:20.180 --> 01:20:23.000
but I'll invite all of you who
want to stay to come on down

01:20:23.000 --> 01:20:26.750
and speak with Professor
Mandelbrot for a few minutes.

01:20:26.750 --> 01:20:29.810
And I'd like to conclude
by thanking Professor

01:20:29.810 --> 01:20:31.820
Lorenz for joining us today.

01:20:31.820 --> 01:20:33.620
[APPLAUSE]

01:20:33.620 --> 01:20:36.920
And let's give thanks
to Professor Mandelbrot.

01:20:36.920 --> 01:20:42.370
[APPLAUSE]

